    "complementary.json",          # RESEARCH (complementary.py): the honest test of whether the dMaSIF surface layer can be FUSED into the ΔΔG-binding node -- i.e. does feeding dMaSIF's geodesic surface embedding at the mutation site as extra features improve held-out ΔΔG magnitude prediction. LEAKAGE-CONTROLLED: dMaSIF trained ONLY on TRAIN complexes, ΔΔG scored on held-out TEST complexes, 3 random splits, node-only baseline on the SAME splits. RESULT (82 complexes, 2,456 alanine mutations): node-only Pearson 0.542 -> node+dMaSIF 0.476 (Δ -0.066) -- fusing them HURTS. The reason is not a bug: flex_physics is a BINDING (extrinsic) predictor and dMaSIF is ALSO extrinsic surface-complementarity, so the 32-d embedding re-expresses interface geometry the node already encodes (buried vdW/contacts) and just adds variance the RF overfits. CONCLUSION: the two extrinsic layers are REDUNDANT, not complementary -- the honest architecture is a PIPELINE (dMaSIF LOCALISES which interface/partner; the ΔΔG node scores the MAGNITUDE of a specific substitution) or, better, an INTRINSIC x EXTRINSIC pairing (folding-stability sensor AND binding sensor), NOT fusion-of-two-extrinsics. This is the empirical basis for the NEXUS dual-sensor design
    "dmasif.json",                 # RESEARCH (dmasif.py, 'dmasif' syscall): Geodesic Surface Learning (the dMaSIF approach) -- the fine surface-POINT representation flagged as the remaining gap to MaSIF-grade after surface_apbs. Primitives are SURFACE POINTS (not residue centres), from a marching-cubes molecular surface with oriented normals; each point carries real APBS Poisson-Boltzmann potential (sampled AT the point) + local curvature + nearest-residue chemistry; the model is a learnable QUASI-GEODESIC CONVOLUTION -- for each point, gather K nearest surface points, express them in the point's LOCAL TANGENT FRAME (two tangent axes + normal), MLP over [local-coords, distance, neighbour-features], Gaussian-weighted by a LEARNED range, stacked twice -> per-point embedding; trained contrastively (true interface point-pairs close, non-contacting far), validated on complexes held OUT by GroupKFold. RESULT on 70 complexes: a REAL, large improvement -- DISCRIMINATION AUC 0.846 (vs residue-patch+APBS 0.76, vs coarse residue 0.65), and DECISIVELY it FIXES retrieval, the test the residue model essentially failed (top-5 ~1.4x chance): the geodesic model pinpoints the actual contacting point at top-1 7.5% (chance 0.4%) / top-5 23.2% (chance 1.8%) = ~13x chance. So the honest arc is clean: mock (fake 99%) -> coarse residue AUC 0.66 / retrieval ~chance -> +APBS+surface 0.76 -> geodesic surface points 0.85 / retrieval 13x -- each step a real measured gain from the right representation/physics. HONEST LIMITS: subsampled surface points (~1500/complex), a compact 2-layer geodesic net on CPU (not full multi-scale dMaSIF at native density on GPU), marching-cubes Gaussian surface (not exact MSMS), a 70-complex subset
    "surface_apbs.json",           # RESEARCH (surface_apbs.py, 'surface apbs' syscall): adds the two things the coarse surface model (surface_fingerprint, AUC 0.66) lacked and the user named -- a real MOLECULAR SURFACE and real ELECTROSTATICS -- using the real tools installed here: pdb2pqr (AMBER charges + protonation) -> APBS (Poisson-Boltzmann solve) -> potential grid sampled at each patch (this IS the APBS the proposal asked for), and a marching-cubes molecular surface over an atomic Gaussian-density grid (the dMaSIF-style substitute for the MSMS binary, which is not installable here). CONTROLLED head-to-head: identical patch framework, identical complex-held-out GroupKFold split, identical contrastive encoder as surface_fingerprint -- the ONLY change is enriching each patch feature vector with the APBS potential + molecular-surface geometry, so any AUC change is attributable to the real physics. RESULT (scaled to ALL data): coarse features 0.652 -> +APBS electrostatics+surface 0.760 (+0.109) held-out on 333 complexes (the full SKEMPI structure set, fetched) -- and the gain GREW with data (was +0.068 on 60): the coarse model plateaus ~0.65 regardless of data (feature-limited), while the APBS+surface model scales to 0.76, so the real physics DOES help and benefits more from more data. HONEST LIMITS: coarse APBS grid (dime 65) for speed; Gaussian marching-cubes surface, not an exact MSMS solvent-excluded surface; 333 of 345 complexes (12 failed pdb2pqr/APBS); residue-centre patches, not per-surface-point geodesic patches (the remaining gap to MaSIF-grade). The tooling is now REAL; installing apbs (apt) + pdb2pqr/scikit-image (pip) is required to re-run
    "desolv_eef1.json",            # RESEARCH (desolv_eef1.py, 'desolv' syscall): retest of an implicit-DESOLVATION term done the RIGHT way, after my first crude occlusion desolvation HURT the model (0.528->0.515). Uses REAL Shrake-Rupley SASA (Bio.PDB), bound-in-complex vs unbound-isolated-chain, for the burial area, PLUS the 'hydrophobic-illusion' SATISFACTION correction: a buried polar/charged atom is only taxed the desolvation cost when it is UNSATISFIED (no H-bond/salt partner N/O within 3.5A across the interface); a satisfied one gets the tax waived. HONEST result on the complex-held-out alanine task (n=1156): the physics VALIDATES -- hydrophobic-buried area tracks measured ΔΔG at r +0.38, and the satisfaction split works (satisfied-polar-buried r +0.25 vs UNSATISFIED-polar-buried r -0.05, two distinct signals the crude term collapsed). It also FIXES the crude term's flaw -- it no longer hurts. BUT it does NOT improve held-out accuracy: baseline (research+sterics+elec/induction) 0.522 -> +EEF1-desolv 0.522 (+0.000), because the hydrophobic-burial signal is already captured by the vdW + buried-contact features, and the alanine scan (removing a sidechain) is a weak place for desolvation to add orthogonal value -- its designed strength (penalizing a mutation that INTRODUCES a buried unsatisfied polar group) is best probed on to-polar mutations with mutant SASA (future work). So: a correct, more physical, no-longer-harmful term, NOT folded into the headline feature set (0.528 stays) -- discipline of only keeping what measurably helps. Compute: real SASA ~0.5-1s/complex, cached
    "surface_fingerprint.json",    # RESEARCH (surface_fingerprint.py, 'surface' syscall): the HONEST, REAL version of a MaSIF-style surface-DL model, built after a user-supplied "SurfaceDLFramework" was shown to be a MOCK (random weight matrix on np.random.uniform data, fabricated ENSG IDs, a rigged apples-to-oranges "speedup", and 99% "confidence" that was an artifact of a rank-6 bottleneck -- garbage inputs scored 99% too). This replaces it with REAL surface patches computed from REAL atomic coordinates + REAL residue chemistry of REAL interface-labelled complexes (58,121 patches, 139 complexes, 13,921 true contacting patch-pairs), a REAL contrastive encoder TRAINED by gradient descent (torch), VALIDATED on complexes held OUT by GroupKFold (now scaled to 344 complexes / 134,745 patches after fetching the full SKEMPI structure set). TWO-SIDED honest result: (WORKS) pairwise surface-COMPLEMENTARITY discrimination -- trained held-out AUC 0.65 vs 0.50 for the untrained raw-feature baseline, so training earns +0.15 of real generalising signal (NOTE: unchanged from the 139-complex run -- more DATA alone does not move the coarse ceiling; feature quality does, which is why APBS+surface below helps); (MOSTLY DOESN'T) exact partner-patch RETRIEVAL among hard decoys is only WEAKLY above chance (top-1 2.2% vs 1.5%, top-5 11.2% vs 7.7% -- a consistent ~1.4x lift, not solved), because pinpointing the one right patch needs finer-than-residue resolution. LIMITS driving the ceiling: residue-level features (geometry-from-atoms + chemistry-from-identity), NO MSMS molecular-surface mesh and NO APBS electrostatics -> coarser than real MaSIF; trained on the 139 real complexes we have (complementarity needs COMPLEXES for labels; the 20k-monomer "proteome DB" is a downstream fetch that wouldn't change this accuracy); surface complementarity is near-field STRUCTURE, NOT validated novel-PPI discovery. The point: the truth (0.66 AUC, at-chance retrieval) is less impressive than the mock's fiction, and that gap is the lesson
    "ppi_screen.json",             # RESEARCH (ppi_screen.py, 'screen' syscall): the ΔΔG node turned into a PPI PARTNER-SCREENING pass, validated honestly by DECOMPOSING screening-for-loss into two separable jobs. (1) LOCALISATION -- 'which candidate partner's interface is this residue on' -- is near-EXACT (top-1 98% of 2,975 interface residues in >=3-chain complexes vs 25% random), but that is pure geometry / near-field STRUCTURE (reading the structure), a reliable LAYER not a hard prediction, so it is reported as a sanity check, not headlined. (2) MAGNITUDE -- 'how much does THIS substitution change binding' -- is the real problem, and the key honest finding is that the localisation/burial score ALONE does NOT predict it: across mixed substitutions burial vs measured |ΔΔG| is r -0.01 (~0) because burial ignores what you mutate TO; fix the substitution (X->Ala) and the on-target signal reappears (r 0.51), and burial cleanly separates the FUNCTIONAL interface (SKEMPI core: burial 1.5, measured |ΔΔG| 1.97) from surface (burial 0.1, |ΔΔG| 0.27) -- so the usable screen = LOCALISATION (structure, exact) x MAGNITUDE (the full ΔΔG model, Pearson ~0.52). (3) GAIN, stated plainly: affinity-increasing mutations are real (6% of SKEMPI ΔΔG<-0.5) and scorable at an EXISTING interface, but a NOVEL emergent partner is NOT claimed -- it needs docking the mutant onto each candidate (unreliable) + experiment. Net: a good LOSS/specificity screen (flag which known partner a mutation breaks and which it spares), an honest NO on novel-partner discovery -- the dynamics-side problem the project keeps finding does not compose
    "interface_hotspots.json",     # RESEARCH (interface_hotspots.py, 'interface' syscall): the pattern INSIDE PPIs at the amino-acid level, for the single-nucleotide-mutation goal. From SKEMPI 2.0 (4,956 MEASURED single-point interface mutations across 345 real complex structures, each with the change in binding affinity ΔΔG and, for ~1,400, on/off rates): (1) POSITION carries the energy -- buried CORE interface residues mean |ΔΔG| 1.92 kcal/mol (38% hotspots >2) vs support 1.48 / rim 0.84 / surface 0.29 (~0% hotspots) = the classic hotspot/O-ring law reproduced (binding energy concentrated in a few buried residues); (2) IDENTITY -- the strongest hotspot residues are aromatic/charged (Y,R,K,F,D,W,L,E), small/flexible ones (S,V,Q,A) weak; (3) BARRIER vs STABILITY (1,382 with kinetics) -- a mutation weakens binding ~68% by destabilising the bound state (off-rate) and ~32% by raising the association barrier (on-rate), separating an energy-barrier effect from an induced-fit/stability effect; (4) PREDICT ΔΔG from residue-change + interface-depth at complex-held-out Pearson 0.36 (honest for simple features; SotA structural predictors ~0.5-0.6 with real burial/contacts -> headroom). So we can flag load-bearing interface positions where a variant will change the interaction, and whether via barrier or stability -- a genuine near-field capability. LIMITS: r~0.36 = triage not exact; SKEMPI biased to studied complexes; needs a solved/predicted COMPLEX structure to place the residue at an interface
    "pathway_struct.json",         # STRUCTURAL dossier for the steps of a known pathway (glycolysis) from REAL data (pathway_struct.py, 'pstruct' syscall): per ordered step -- gene(s)/isoenzymes, reaction (substrate->product, before/after), protein FAMILY/fold (UniProt), residue-level ACTIVE-SITE + ligand-binding residues (UniProt ft_act_site/ft_binding, e.g. GAPDH catalytic Cys152 nucleophile, TPI1 electrophile@96), PPI partners (cell DB), oligomeric state + allosteric regulation (UniProt), AND an AlphaFold structural analysis with a CROSS-CHECKED induced-fit signal: split the fold into two lobes, require both a split active site AND genuinely separated lobes (gap + gap/Rg) -> recovers the canonical DOMAIN-CLOSURE enzymes (HK1 66Å lobe gap, PGK1, + multidomain PKM/GPI on top); the gap criterion correctly demotes PGAM1 (compact single domain the raw span false-positived); HONESTLY DISCLOSED false negative: no domain-split metric sees LOOP-closure induced fit, so TPI1 (catalytic loop-6) scores flat. Sources: UniProt REST + AlphaFold DB (v6 via API) + cell DB, all cached in scratchpad. The trustworthy NEAR-FIELD structural layer; does NOT predict far-field consequence of perturbing a step
    "latent_bridge_test.json",     # test of the proposed Mechanistic-Latent-Mechanistic (M-L-M) architecture's load-bearing assumption BEFORE wiring a real foundation model (latent_bridge_test.py, 'latent' syscall): can a Geneformer/scGPT-style latent (faithful co-expression-SVD gene embedding + k-NN response transfer) predict a HELD-OUT knockout's response and beat 'predict the mean perturbation response'? RESULT on 200 held-out knockouts: LATENT AUC 0.711 vs MEAN baseline 0.693 = gain only +0.018 (and MEAN WINS on correlation, 0.537 vs 0.518). Decisively, stratified by novelty: for NOVEL perturbations (far from any training gene -- the pitch's claimed moat) the latent is WORSE than the mean (gain -0.017); it only helps when a close twin is already in training (+0.04). So Phase 2 does NOT cross the transitivity wall -- it relocates it into latent space as 'this perturbation is unlike anything in training'. Consistent with published benchmarks (Ahlmann-Eltze 2024: scGPT/Geneformer/GEARS don't beat simple baselines on perturbation prediction). Foundation model worth wiring for INTERPOLATION (seen perturbations/new context -- the cross-cell-type xcell already does this at r~0.6), NOT for novel-missense whole-cell prediction
    "fieldsim_test.json",          # THE HONEST TEST of fieldsim: run real gene knockouts through the engine's forward model (regulatory Hill+decay propagation, Layers 2+4) and score the predicted downstream signature vs the MEASURED Perturb-seq effect (fieldsim_test.py, 'fieldsim validate'). RESULT on 52 knockouts with >=25 real measured movers: fieldsim ranks which genes move at AUC ~0.50 = CHANCE, and no better than a STATIC baseline of just listing the knockout's curated targets (AUC ~0.50) -- the Hill+decay+diffusion DYNAMICS add nothing over the network topology. Root cause = coverage: fieldsim only reaches genes on a curated regulatory path (~3% of the genes that actually moved) while lighting up a ~500-gene neighborhood that mostly didn't move (huge false reach, ~0 recall); and on the few movers it reaches, direction is below a coin-flip (sign agreement ~0.16). Same far-field-does-not-compose wall as transitivity ~0.009. CONCLUSION: fieldsim is a correct mechanistic DEMONSTRATOR whose knockout prediction does NOT beat chance/a network lookup; the measured knockout/cascade engines remain the trustworthy tools
    "fieldsim.json",               # the FOUR-LAYER continuous 'protein-field' whole-cell engine (fieldsim.py, 'fieldsim' syscall) built end-to-end from a shared architecture proposal and tested HONESTLY: L1 SH field overlap via coefficient contraction (== direct integral to 0.1%, co-centred only) + L2 mutation->ΔΔG->decay-rate γ + L3 SDF reaction-diffusion PDE (nucleus/mito/membrane, harmonic-mean face conductance) + L4 Hill-gated source, run as a closed loop (TF knockout collapses downstream enzyme field 100%; SDF confines a field 100% without an export motif vs 28% with). It ASSEMBLES AND RUNS and is internally consistent, but the tests quantify why it is a MECHANISTIC DEMONSTRATOR not a predictor: (1) the single-centre SH 'seam' is real -- a co-centred source is 1 coefficient but needs ~121 once 0.34 off-centre, and re-centring is an O(L^3) FMM translation, so diffusing the SH object is the wrong move (keep c_lm static+co-centred, diffuse a scalar); (2) the proposed γ=γ_wt·exp(ΔΔG/RT) diverges to unphysical rates (2.1e7 at ΔΔG=10) -> replaced with a bounded folded-fraction form; (3) heavy external models (ESMFold/FoldX/APBS/Enformer) are transparent stand-ins, and per every measured result here (transitivity ~0.009) the whole-cell mutation->phenotype map does NOT compose from local fields. Build it to study the mechanism/generate hypotheses; check every edge vs measured data
    "xaira_coverage.json",         # staged the Xaira X-Atlas/Orion genome-wide Perturb-seq screen as a SECOND cell type (fetch_xaira.py streams the 8M-cell HCT116 dataset from HuggingFace one parquet batch at a time -> pseudobulk [pert x gene] signature -> {SCRATCH}/hct116.h5ad; peak disk ~1 batch). COVERAGE this adds: knockouts of cell-model genes 8,696 (K562) -> 15,749 UNION = 95.4% of the cell perturbed in >=1 cell type (+7,053 genes K562 never knocked out); 8,288 knockouts now in BOTH cell types (cross-cell-validatable); genes-measured readout 6,977 -> 16,383 (2.3x, Xaira sequences ~16k UMIs/cell). VALIDATED cross-cell-type consistency: on strongly-moved genes a knockout's HCT116 signature matches its K562 signature at Pearson 0.611 (shuffled 0.064) -- the same knockout does the same thing across leukemia & colorectal cells. Powers the `xcell` syscall (crosscell.py): cell-type-ROBUST vs cell-type-specific responders (PSMB5 proteostasis = robust r0.55; GATA1 hematopoietic TF = 0 robust, K562-only -- correctly cell-type-specific). NOTE: hct116.h5ad (1.1GB) lives in scratchpad (ephemeral, regenerated by fetch_xaira.py), not committed
    "pertseq_apps.json",           # the FOUR canonical Perturb-seq applications attempted on this K562 data, each validated & honestly graded (pertseq_apps.py, 'pertseq' syscall): [1] GRN WIRING **does NOT work on this data** -- inferred TF->TF influence edges recover curated SIGNED direct regulation only 1.6x chance with 48% sign agreement (=chance) and FFLs are 52% coherent (=chance) = a CO-RESPONSE network, not causal wiring; a single bulk steady-state condition can't separate direct from indirect (needs time-resolved/nascent-RNA data). [2] GENE FUNCTION by transcriptomic fingerprint **WORKS** -- fingerprint-kNN recovers a held-out known gene's pathway 84% (4.1x chance), 128 confident dark-gene calls (TIMM23B->mito import, GPN3->RNA-pol assembly); a validated similarity HINT (can be fooled by shared growth-arrest phenotype), 'pertseq function GENE'. [3] DISEASE CONVERGENCE method validated (spliceosome positive control z=156) but thin (only 1 disease has enough knocked-out risk genes; K562 wrong context for neuro/immune disease). [4] IMMUNE brakes-vs-gas NOT feasible (needs +/- stimulus axis + immune cells; this is one unstimulated leukemia condition). Honest scorecard: one works, one validated hint, one method-awaiting-data, one out-of-scope
    "influence.json",              # perturbation used for ONE thing (influence.py, 'cascade'/'trace' syscall): a directed network of what a knockout AFFECTS (the measured ENDS -- we know the endpoints, not the mechanism), then each effect's ROUTE reconstructed WITHOUT predicting anything unmeasured -- DIRECT (X physically/causally contacts P), MEASURED-MEDIATED (a specific non-hub stepping-stone M that is itself a measured knockout: X->M->P, both hops real data -- this is where the OTHER knockouts are kept in context), or UNRESOLVED. VALIDATED honest on this screen: ~1.2% direct (6.5x chance), ~14% mediated (43x a placebo -- stepping-stones are non-random), ~85% unresolved (far-field that doesn't compose). The influence network is 100% real data; the route is reconstructable for a validated MINORITY. CONFOUND CONTROLLED: pan-hub mediators (whose KO moves everything) excluded, and PPI/pathway structure used only to name the DIRECT entry point (as a mediator filter it just re-selects hubs and doesn't help)
    "protein_knockout.json",       # knock out the PROTEIN, not the gene (protein_knockout.py, 'protein'/'degrade' syscall): degrading a protein (PROTAC-style) removes it from EVERY complex it's a subunit of at once -> the STRUCTURAL disassembly (SMARCA4 collapses all 4 SWI/SNF variants, PSMA1 all 4 proteasome assemblies), leaving co-subunits expressed but their assembly incomplete. VALIDATED honest: the STRONG signal is structural -- 46% of complex proteins have a complex-mate among their top co-dependencies (vs 0.04% chance, ~1000x), machine parts are co-essential; the measured TRANSCRIPTIONAL signal is deliberately WEAK (median 1.07x mate movement, only 18% of machines >1.5x) because a protein KO acts POST-transcriptionally and mRNA-seq is mostly blind to it (proteasome 3.1x / spliceosome are the feedback-wired exceptions) -- which is exactly why the structural view is NOT redundant with the measured gene `knockout`. LIMIT: one-protein-per-gene (no isoform/PTM/domain-resolved network -> no isoform-specific KO); names the machines that break, not the far-field cascade
    "graph_label.json",            # INTEGRATION (graph_label.py, 'kg' syscall): the LABELED multi-relational cell graph -- nodes = genes, edges TYPED by relation, adding the two relations CellGraph lacked: PATHWAY co-membership (Reactome, capped <=50 genes/pathway so 'Metabolism'-type hubs don't hairball) + LITERATURE co-mention (litmine: two genes share a PMID), alongside PPI. Built: 16,492 nodes, 15,103 with >=1 labeled edge, 383,246 unique labeled pairs -- ppi 191,447 (14,230 genes) + path 208,881 (7,282 genes) + lit 4,395 (2,677 genes). Cross-type overlap ppi&path 0.11, ppi&lit 0.03, path&lit 0.00 -> the three relations are COMPLEMENTARY (each says a DIFFERENT thing about a gene pair, not redundant). 'kg GENE' shows a gene's typed neighbourhood (HOW it connects: physical / same-pathway / co-mentioned). HONEST SCOPE: a DESCRIPTIVE near-field knowledge graph -- these edge types predict a knockout's effect at ~floor (r 0.03-0.09; pathway 0.064; only curated complexes 0.23), so it is the substrate for representation/link-prediction/querying, NOT a causal/phenotype predictor
    "spatial_ladder.json",         # INTEGRATION (spatial_ladder.py, 'ladder' syscall): a pathway laid out by COMPARTMENT (localization x pathway), so you can see which of its proteins act at the membrane, in the cytoplasm, and in the nucleus. Built to test the user's 'ladder' idea -- a pathway that runs membrane->nucleus would tell you which reactions happen where, in order. MEASURED NEGATIVE up front (the reason it's honest): the directional ladder is NOT real -- nuclear vs membrane signalling genes have near-identical regulatory-tier means (diff -0.039, ~0) and there are MORE TFs among UPSTREAM genes (24%) than downstream (8%), the opposite of a membrane->nucleus flow; the cell_levels 'tier' is a regulatory-graph order, not a spatial one, and every large pathway spans all four compartments anyway. So NO directional route is claimed. What IS delivered: a per-pathway compartment LAYOUT read directly from localization (~100% coverage). Proteins are MULTI-compartment (the source lists every compartment a protein is seen in, NOT primacy-ordered -- HRAS is tagged Nucleus+Golgi+membrane+cytoplasm at once), so each protein is counted in EVERY layer it's annotated in (not one arbitrary pick) -> a compartment PROFILE/occupancy view + per-layer gene lists, depth-ordered outside->in for readability only. 'ladder PATHWAY' (fuzzy name) shows one pathway's layers, 'ladder GENE' shows a gene's compartments + its pathways, 'ladder' shows the reality-check. HONEST SCOPE: curated/observed annotations joined together (near-field descriptive), NOT a phenotype predictor and NOT a claimed reaction-localization
    "lit_place.json",              # INTEGRATION (lit_place.py, extends the 'ladder' syscall for ORPHAN genes): place a gene NOT in our pathway data (a dark / orphan gene -- 4,708 of them have litmine abstracts) using its LITERATURE. Two halves: (1) COMPARTMENT read DIRECTLY from localization (~100% coverage, dark genes included) -- solid, no prediction; (2) FUNCTIONAL CONTEXT inferred by mining the gene's abstracts for co-mentioned KNOWN genes (case-sensitive symbol regex + English-word stoplist) and transferring THOSE genes' Reactome pathways via an IDF-weighted vote (IDF down-weights promiscuous hubs like TP53 that co-occur with everyone) -> a ranked candidate-pathway HYPOTHESIS. VALIDATED leave-one-out on KNOWN genes (build a known-known co-mention graph from the abstracts, hide a gene's pathway, predict from co-mentioned neighbours): pathway top-1 ~0.13 / top-5 ~0.21 vs random-from-candidate-pool ~0.055 = ~3.5-4x chance (IDF-weighted beats raw/binary). So co-mention is a REAL but NOISY pathway signal -- a hypothesis GENERATOR (right pathway in the top-5 about 1 in 5 times), NOT a confident annotation and NOT a phenotype predictor; compartment (the other half) is solid because it's read, not predicted. Demonstrated live and CHECKABLE: FNDC5(irisin) -> UCP1/PGC-1a/BDNF -> 'brown & beige adipocyte differentiation / mitochondrial uncoupling' (correct thermogenesis biology); STRA8/DAZL (germ-cell) -> 'Meiotic recombination' (STRA8 is the meiosis gatekeeper); TNFRSF19(TROY)/SP5 -> 'Signaling by WNT / beta-catenin' (both genuine Wnt genes). Wired into 'ladder GENE': an orphan gene now returns its known compartment + literature co-mentions + candidate function, so the compartment-ladder covers genes with no pathway annotation. HONEST SCOPE: curated regex mining (not full NER), single litmine source (<=6 papers/gene), descriptive near-field -- a starting hypothesis for an uncharacterised gene, to be confirmed experimentally
    "connect.json",                # INTEGRATION (connect.py, 'connect' syscall): CONNECT the three orphan-gene signals -- literature (lit_place candidate pathway), PPI (physical partners from cell_complete["ppi"], 191k edges, 80% of orphan+lit genes have >=1), and spatial (localization) -- into ONE mechanistic hypothesis card, and cross-check them against each other. The load-bearing idea (the user's): if the literature says pathway X, find the gene's physical partners, see where they come from and what reactions they run, verify the locations, and flag a protein that could carry the gene between compartments. VALIDATED, held-out on KNOWN genes (n=5,409), and the WIN is cross-source AGREEMENT: pathway top-1 precision lit-only 0.125, ppi-only 0.538, CORROBORATED (a pathway that BOTH the literature co-mention AND a physical partner point to) 0.706 -- 5.6x lit-only, firing for ~18% of genes -> when two independent sources agree the call jumps from noisy to 71%-precise. Spatial cross-check is grounded: PPI partners share >=1 compartment 0.927 vs 0.507 random = 1.83x (interacting proteins co-localize), so verifying an orphan's location against its partners is sound, and the ~7% that DON'T co-localize are exactly where a transport step is needed. Transport reasoning = a HEURISTIC lead (clearly flagged): when the gene and its inferred reaction sit in different compartments, flag a partner with proc in {transport/uptake, trafficking/secretion} whose compartments bridge the gap. CHECKABLE demos: WDHD1(AND-1) -> CORROBORATED 'DNA strand elongation / unwinding' with its MCM2-7 helicase partners, gap=cytoplasm bridged by KPNA1 (importin-a imports replication factors) -- textbook fork biology; CSE1L(CAS) -> nuclear-transport context via partners KPNA1/NUP50, carrier KPNA1 (CSE1L is literally the exportin that recycles importin-a) -- correct machinery; UNC13C -> 'Neurotransmitter Release Cycle' via UNC13B (Munc13 vesicle priming); STRA8 -> honestly NO corroboration (its partners don't match the meiosis literature) so it falls back to lit-only, not a forced call. Confidence TIERS keep it honest: measured PPI > corroborated > lit-hypothesis > transport-heuristic. HONEST SCOPE: descriptive near-field triangulation -- a strong STARTING mechanism (corroborated ~71% precision) to test experimentally, NOT a proven annotation and NOT a phenotype predictor
    "trrust_regulon.json",         # DATA (fetched, TRRUST v2 via grnpedia.org): literature-curated human TF->target regulon, 795 TFs / 9,396 signed edges (Activation/Repression/Unknown) each PubMed-backed. Fetched to improve the graph. MEASURED role: helps condition inference (top-3 80% vs raw reg 76%) but does NOT beat the which-targets-move wall (1.1x, like reg) -- a curated edge still may not fire in a given cell context. Merged into cell_conditions alongside SIGNOR (the best source: 2.7x which-moves, 88% condition top-3). The honest lesson from the data experiment: edge QUALITY (SIGNOR curated causal) beats quantity, but the far-field wall is informational (needs measured context-specific function), not fixed by more annotation regulons
    "perturb_recall.json",         # RESEARCH (perturb_recall.py): the honest test of "a neural net + all the data will get us RECALL" on which genes move when a gene is knocked out, held-out BY KNOCKOUT on measured Perturb-seq (K562, 9,871 KOs x 8,202 genes). Gradient-boosted classifier fusing base-rate (response-proneness) + graph-neighbour co-response transfer + reg/SIGNOR targets + PPI. MEASURED (545 held-out KOs, mean AUPRC / recall@50): random 0.017/0.4%, graph-edges-alone 0.019/3.2% (~useless -- matches the 3% wall), base-rate 0.152/15%, transfer 0.155/15%, MODEL 0.183/18%. HONEST: the model WORKS (11x random, beats every baseline) but recall is PARTIAL (top-50 captures ~18% of movers) and the lift OVER the simple base-rate baseline is modest (+0.03 AUPRC). Most predictable recall is "which genes are response-prone" + "what my network-neighbour knockouts do"; the graph edges alone are ~random and knockout-SPECIFIC prediction stays hard. Consistent with the field (foundation models barely beat baselines on perturbation prediction) and this project's latent-bridge (+0.018 over mean). More data/screens raise base-rate QUALITY but do not break the wall on this evidence -- the missing signal is measured context-specific function, not model capacity. A real triage LIFT (3% -> 18% recall@50), not a solved cascade
    "tfbs_score.json",             # PRODUCT (tfbs_score.py, 'tfbs' syscall): the honest protein-DNA analogue of NEXUS's binding node -- score how a DNA variant changes a TF's binding SITE. Built the RIGHT way for the different chemistry (4 bases, phosphate-backbone electrostatics, groove/shape readout -- NOT amino-acid rotamer physics), using the mature measured-data method: JASPAR2024 PWM log-odds (743 vertebrate TFs, tf_motifs.json, from HT-SELEX/ChIP) over a sliding window on both strands. NEIGHBOURING NUCLEOTIDES are accounted for (the motif window spans flanks; a variant's effect depends on core-vs-flank position -- demoed: ARNT E-box CACGTG, a core C->A drops the score 7.8->3.4 = abolishes site, a flank change ~0). 'tfbs GENE [SEQ [POS ALT]]'. HONEST SCOPE: predicts INTRINSIC in-vitro sequence PREFERENCE (validated by construction; literature AUC ~0.85-0.9), NOT in-vivo binding/regulation (chromatin/cofactor/TF-concentration = the same context wall). DNA SHAPE ('rotation' -- minor groove width/roll/twist/propeller) and dinucleotide dependencies are the documented Rohs add-on (a modest validated bump, needs the pentamer shape table). KEY POINT: this is a NEW motif/shape engine, NOT a retrofit of NEXUS's protein physics -- the right honest answer to 'make NEXUS work on DNA'. Useful for non-coding / regulatory variant interpretation (does a variant disrupt a TF site), not for predicting the downstream gene in a given cell
    "cofactor.json",               # PRODUCT (cofactor.py, 'cofactor' syscall): the honest solution to the COFACTOR-SWITCH problem -- a TF binds a DIFFERENT sequence depending on which partner it dimerises with (SMAD3+FOXH1 vs SMAD3+RUNX; FLI1 alone binds ETS but FLI1+CEBPB binds a fused composite). Solved via the DATA route, NOT the compute-blocked structural route: fetched JASPAR2024 composite (heterodimer) motifs (composite_motifs.json, 479 TF1::TF2 pairs, measured by HT-SELEX of the DIMER) = the sequence the PAIR actually binds; gated by which cofactor is EXPRESSED in a cell (emask, 200 types) + physical pairing (PPI). MEASURED/VALIDATED the switch is real: composite motifs are longer/distinct from either solo -- mean 14.4bp vs 10.6bp, 82% of composites longer than either partner's solo motif; concrete fused half-sites (FLI1::CEBPB = acCGGAAGT+TGCGCAAt, ATF3::FLI1 = ETS+TGACGTCA, GATA1::TAL1 = GATA+E-box). Context-gating demoed: FLI1 in monocyte -> CEBPB/CEBPD available (myeloid), in T cell -> BHLHE40, in B cell -> TCF4/ZBTB20 -> different composite site each. 'cofactor TF [CELLTYPE]' lists expressed partners; 'cofactor TF1 TF2' shows composite-vs-solo. HONEST SCOPE: delivers the pair's IN-VITRO binding site (the concrete switch, measured), NOT (a) de-novo complex formation from structure/affinity -- the AF-Multimer + absolute-affinity route we DELIBERATELY SKIP (compute-blocked in sandbox, unreliable, and cofactor choice is driven more by concentration/cooperativity than raw dG anyway), nor (b) the in-vivo wall (whether the pair binds a given promoter and regulates it = chromatin/context). Limited to the ~479 pairs with a measured composite motif. A real data-grounded cofactor-specificity layer for variant reasoning, not a de-novo complex predictor
    "invivo_gate.json",            # PRODUCT (invivo_gate.py, 'invivo' syscall; data via fetch_invivo.py): take a TF motif scan IN VIVO by gating 1D sequence hits through REAL ENCODE K562 chromatin, then MEASURE the payoff against ground truth. The standard pipeline, executed and validated on a real chromosome (chr22) slice: [Gate1] intersect PWM hits with DNase/ATAC peaks (open chromatin -- a TF can't bind nucleosomal DNA); [Gate2] intersect survivors with H3K27ac peaks (active enhancer/promoter); [Gate3] map the bound enhancer to its target GENE via ABC/Hi-C 3D links (ENCODE-rE2G ENCFF902VSE), NOT linear proximity. GROUND TRUTH = ENCODE K562 TF ChIP-seq (12 TFs, never trained on) = where the TF ACTUALLY binds. MEASURED (chr22, precision = predicted sites that are truly bound): mean precision 0.27 (1D sequence-only) -> 0.66 (open+active), 2.4x. The result is motif-length-dependent and confirms the theory: DEGENERATE short motifs (GATA1 7bp 1.6%->50%, YY1 8bp 28x, GATA2 19x, MAX 8x) gain enormously -- gating kills ~94-99% of their sequence-only false positives (GATA1 7361 hits -> 76, and precision up 20x); LONG specific motifs (CTCF 15bp 69%, REST 20bp 100%, NRF1 12bp 62%) are already precise from sequence alone (motif is rare by chance), so gating adds little (1.0-1.4x). Gate3 measured the user's point directly: of GATA1's 56 open+active sites with an ABC target, the 3D target GENE differs from the linear-nearest gene 39% of the time (e.g. a GATA1 site near YPEL1 loops to MAPK1) -- nearest-neighbour guessing is wrong that often. HONEST SCOPE: converts an in-vitro sequence preference (tfbs_score PWM) into an in-vivo BINDING prediction and shows, on measured ChIP, how much each gate helps -- one-chromosome slice of a genome-wide method (whole genome needs the 3Gb reference; method identical). It predicts where the TF PHYSICALLY LANDS in this cell, well; it does NOT predict which genes MOVE on knockout (binding != regulation -- the separate dynamics wall this project keeps hitting). The recall side is honest too: at this stringent threshold motif->ChIP recall is low (many real ChIP peaks lack a strong canonical motif = indirect/tethered binding), so gating buys precision at the cost of recall. All REAL ENCODE K562 data; nothing simulated
