    "seq_model_full.json",        # RESEARCH (seq_model.py main_full + build_pretrain.py + tier2_seq_model.ipynb): the FULL Tier-2 architecture (the version the user specced, after the reduced 0.488 one). Shared conv encoder PRETRAINED on 100k genome-wide ENCODE cCREs to predict 311-TF ChIP binding FROM SEQUENCE (the dense aux task that solves data starvation), THEN fine-tuned with the sparse CRISPR head; compared WITH vs WITHOUT pretraining on Colab GPU, chromosome-held-out AUPRC + shuffle control. RESULT: pretraining SUCCEEDED (aux TF-binding-from-sequence AUC 0.77 -- the encoder genuinely learned the motif grammar) but did NOT transfer to regulation: pretrained fine-tune 0.479 ~ from-scratch 0.491 (within noise), both < epigenetics-only GBM 0.519 << TF-identity GBM 0.608; shuffle control 0.074 clean. FAIR COMPLETE NO-GO (nothing crippled this time) so the ceiling is REAL. WHY even done right: (1) cannot out-predict a measurement -- GBM gets 311 TFs binding MEASURED, encoder only PREDICTS it (AUC 0.77 lossy); (2) the fine-tune head already has the MEASURED activity tracks (DNase/H3K27ac/PolII/eRNA) as tabular features and sequence-predicted binding is largely redundant with those; (3) 569 positives cap the head regardless of encoder quality. Matches the field (CRISPR element-gene is dominated by measured activity+3D contact, not pure sequence). Architecture was sound; data reality (measured>>predicted) decides it. Production model = Tier-1 GBM (TF identity, 0.608). Both deep variants (reduced 0.488, full 0.479) honestly lose to the GBM on a fair test -- the deep-learning arc earned its no             # RESEARCH (seq_model.py + tier2_seq_model.ipynb): Tier-2 multi-task sequence CNN for CRISPR element->gene regulation, trained on Colab GPU. Conv motif encoder over 600bp element DNA -> AUX head predicts 311-TF binding from sequence (dense ~1.2M labels, learns the motif grammar) + regulation head on [embedding+8 tabular]. Chromosome-held-out AUPRC, 3 seeds + label-shuffle control. RESULT = honest NO-GO: seq CNN 0.488 (3-seed stable 0.486/0.483/0.496) BEATS distance (0.393, so it reads regulatory sequence better than proximity) but UNDERPERFORMS epigenetics-only GBM (0.519) and the TF-identity GBM (0.608). Label-shuffle control collapses to 0.081 (~base rate 0.055) = legit, no leak/overfit -- just not better. Two reasons: predicting binding FROM sequence is lossy (the GBM gets 311 TFs binding MEASURED), and 569 positives are too few for a from-scratch CNN to learn the combinatorial grammar. VERDICT: ship the Tier-1 GBM (TF identity, 0.608) as the production regulation predictor; the strategy discipline held (adopt deep only if it wins held-out -- it didnt). Revisit Tier-2 only with far more labelled regulation data OR a pretrained sequence foundation model (Enformer/Borzoi embeddings) instead of a scratch CNN. The honest ML deliverable: element->gene regulation at AUPRC ~0.61, SOTA-competitive + leakage-controlled + not over-engineered # RESEARCH (crispr_gate.py regate_compendium/identity_test): the decisive experiment the Tier-1 gate named -- rebuild the TF feature from the FULL ENCODE K562 TF-ChIP compendium (fetched all 311 TFs, 39x the prior 8) and re-gate (chromosome GroupKFold, AUPRC). TWO-PART RESULT. (1) COUNT ceilings: tf_count from 311 TFs (mean 49/element) does NOT grow the margin, it shrinks it (0.495->0.471; a dense count is a noisier active-region proxy than the sparse erythroid count). (2) IDENTITY passes: testing WHICH of 311 TFs bind (311 binary features, leakage-free = no label-based selection) lifts AUPRC to 0.608 (3-seed stable 0.601-0.612) vs 0.519 epigenetics-alone vs 0.393 distance -- and the LABEL-SHUFFLE CONTROL collapses to 0.069 (~base rate 0.055), proving no leak/overfit despite 311 features. So the combinatorial-TF hypothesis is CONFIRMED via identity (which TFs co-bind), not count (how many) -- the count discards the signal identity keeps. VERDICT = PASS/GO for deep learning: a tree over binary TF-occupancy already hits 0.608 (+0.09 over epigenetics, +0.21 over distance), so a sequence model learning TF motif grammar + combinations directly is JUSTIFIED. The gate worked across both rounds: stopped a premature deep-net spend on the wrong feature (count), then gave a clean controlled GO once the right feature (identity) was tested. Feature-level leak check clean (top TFs RNF2/GATA1/FOXM1/RUNX1 = real biology, r~0.25)            # RESEARCH (crispr_gate.py): Tier-1 Go/No-Go for the regulation ML model. Mechanistic K562 feature matrix (distance, element+promoter DNase/H3K27ac/PolII/eRNA signal, TF-ChIP count, gene expr, ABC) -> XGBoost, GroupKFold BY CHROMOSOME, AUPRC, vs distance-only + ABC-only baselines on the same folds (monotonic constraints + scale_pos_weight). 10,331 CRISPR element-gene pairs (ENCFF968BZL), 5.5% base rate. The distance trap is real (hits median 34kb vs non-hits 388kb -> distance-only AUPRC 0.405, the true bar; ABC-only 0.247 GLOBAL is abstention-deflated -- scores only ~8% of pairs, ~0.58 where it fires). Result: XGBoost(raw epi) 0.495 = +0.090 over distance, 4/5 folds. VERIFIED by a 4-lens adversarial workflow (ablation, distance-control, seed-robustness): the +0.09 is REAL orthogonal signal, not distance repackaged -- GBM beats distance WITHIN all 4 distance bins (+0.108/+0.063/+0.028/+0.006), robust across 3 seed reshuffles (+0.095/+0.109/+0.111), chromosome-disjoint folds (no leak). The orthogonal lift is almost entirely tf_count (drop-one -0.042; h3k27ac -0.013 second; epi-only 0.216 = 4x base), and tf_count uses only ~8 ChIP tracks not the ~300-TF ENCODE compendium -> +0.09 is a FLOOR. VERDICT = CONDITIONAL PASS: task is learnable (real verified orthogonal signal beyond distance) but modest (~0.50 AUPRC) and single-feature-dependent; the disciplined next step is NOT deep learning but rebuilding tf_count from the full TF compendium + a feature-level leak check, then re-gate -- a sequence CNN is justified only if that shows the margin grows. The gate did its job: stopped a premature deep-net spend and named the one decisive experiment
    "bind_vs_reg.json",            # PRODUCT (bind_vs_reg.py, 'bindreg' syscall): the direct comparison the whole TF arc pointed at -- for a TF with BOTH a measured BINDING map (ENCODE K562 ChIP-seq, peak->gene via promoter TSS+/-5kb + ABC 3D distal) and a measured REGULATION map (Replogle K562 Perturb-seq knockdown -> genes with |z|>3), put the two gene sets side by side. FIRST honest finding: only GATA1 is well-powered enough to even try -- of ~30 TFs with both ChIP and a Perturb-seq knockdown, only GATA1 has a clean regulation signature (56 genes; MAX marginal; the rest sit at the noise floor because genome-scale Perturb-seq has few cells per knockdown). GATA1: BINDS ~4,621 genes (promoter 1,614 + ABC distal), REGULATES 56, overlap 29. VERIFIED by a 4-lens adversarial workflow + permutation test: the two maps overlap only at CHANCE (0.85x promoter-only, 0.96x +ABC; permutation p~0.7, robust across thresholds/windows). CRUCIAL, VERIFIED nuance -- this is NOT proof that binding is non-functional: the near-chance overlap is mostly a POWER/COMPOSITION artifact. (a) The measured regulated set is ~100% INDIRECT -- all 56 genes go UP on knockdown (a lineage-identity shift; top genes LTB z=40, LST1, CTSC, S100A4 are the myeloid/alternative-lineage program GATA1 does NOT bind), so binding overlaps them at chance because it SHOULD. (b) GATA1's genuinely bound DIRECT erythroid targets (KLF1/TAL1/NFE2/ALAS2/FECH/SLC25A37/TFRC) are ALL promoter-bound but sit at |z|<1 (undetected as regulated) because the knockdown is weak (on-target z=-0.84, zero down-genes). So binding correctly marks the direct regulon; the perturbation just fails to detect it. Real buffering (genuinely non-functional binding) surely exists in general but these data can neither measure nor exclude it. DURABLE FINDINGS: (1) measured binding and measured regulation are largely DISJOINT as observed -- direct targets under-detected, measured regulation dominated by indirect effects; (2) most TFs can't even be tested (Perturb-seq power); (3) directionality is real biology (GATA1 knockdown de-represses the myeloid program = the textbook GATA1<->PU.1 lineage antagonism). A rigorously-verified, honestly-hedged answer to 'compare binding vs regulation': the maps don't line up, but mostly because of what the perturbation can measure, not proven non-function. 'Regulated' mixes direct+indirect. Real data throughout. LITERATURE RESOLUTION (compare_literature, TRRUST PubMed-curated regulon vs ChIP binding, hypergeometric significance): swapping the under-powered Perturb-seq for a well-powered curated regulon FLIPS the result -- binding is SIGNIFICANTLY enriched among curated targets for the well-characterised TFs: GATA1 2.33x p=4.7e-4, MYC 1.61x p=2.3e-4, SPI1 1.36x p=0.018 (GATA2 1.74x but underpowered n=18 ns). GATA1 binding recovers the EXACT direct erythroid regulon the knockdown missed: ALAS2, KLF1, NFE2, EPOR, ITGA2B, GP9, GP6, HEMGN, PPOX, CEBPA, BACH1. So the definitive answer: binding DOES predict regulation when the regulation map is not detection-limited; the Perturb-seq chance-overlap (0.85x) was a POWER artifact, not evidence that binding is non-functional. Honest exceptions: enhancer-acting TFs TAL1 (0.38x) and RUNX1 (0.6x) stay flat -- promoter-only assignment is the wrong lens for them (they act through distal enhancers) and their curated sets are tiny (10, 39). Genome-wide ChIP for GATA1/GATA2/SPI1/MYC/TAL1/KLF1/RUNX1 fetched to chip_gw/
    "cell_conditions.json",        # INTEGRATION (cell_conditions.py, 'conditions' syscall): infer WHEN/why a pathway turns on by tracing its genes BACKWARD to the stress/signal TFs that control them (curated directed reg edges, 612k; hypergeometric enrichment). Implements the subtractive strategy: pathways with NO condition-TF enrichment are CONSTITUTIVE (always-on housekeeping -- translation, rRNA, TCA, splicing all correctly flagged); enriched ones report the TRIGGER via ~16 curated condition-TFs (HIF1A->hypoxia, SREBF1/2->low sterol, ATF4/XBP1/ATF6/DDIT3->ER stress, NFE2L2->oxidative, TP53->DNA damage, NFKB1/RELA->inflammation, STAT1/STAT3->interferon/cytokine, SMAD3->TGF-beta, HSF1->heat, FOXO3->starvation). Deliberately EXCLUDES broad proliferation TFs (MYC/E2F1) whose targets are the constitutive growth machinery -- including them mislabelled housekeeping as 'growth-activated' and outcompeted the specific stress signal (measured failure, then fixed). VALIDATED: on 58 pathways whose condition is stated in the name, the traced condition-TF is recovered top-1 67% / top-3 76% (chance ~7%/20% = ~9x/4x). 'conditions scan' surfaces the LEFTOVER -- pathways whose trigger is NOT in their name but is inferred: TLR2/3/4/7/9 cascades -> NF-kB inflammation, IL-4/13 -> STAT, FOXO-mediated transcription -> starvation (all correct). KEY DISTINCTION: this is a STRUCTURAL/annotation query (enrichment of a curated TF's targets in a gene set), NOT the forward dynamical propagation this project measured fails at chance (directed reg edges predict which targets MOVE at only 1.1x; but the SIGN is 73% and the static regulated-by wiring is real). HONEST LIMITS: condition-CATEGORY resolution (can confuse related stresses, e.g. hypoxia vs ER stress); ~16 curated condition-TFs (major axes, not all -- no circadian/developmental); reg edges are annotation (ChIP+curated = regulated-by, not measured-functional-in-K562). A hypothesis generator for a pathway's trigger, not a proven condition
    "cascade_all.json",            # RESEARCH (cascade_all.py, 'cascadeall'/'thirdarm' syscall): the FAIR combined shot at the third arm -- predict the genome-wide knockout cascade (which genes move) by COMBINING every layer into one supervised model, tested on HELD-OUT knockouts. For each knockout G, score every gene J by P(J moves); features per (G,J) combine ALL layers: regulatory edge/sign, TRRUST regulon, signaling, RWR over the multi-layer graph, PPI-adjacency, complex, coexpression, reaction-network (catalysis-precedes), + J-intrinsic (generic mover-frequency, baseline expression) + G-intrinsic (is-TF, out-degree). XGBoost, GroupKFold BY KNOCKOUT (predict cascades of unseen knockouts). 88 knockouts, 23,040 pairs, base mover rate 6.2%. RESULT (the honest reframing of the wall): the cascade IS predictable -- AUPRC 0.567 (9.08x base) -- but ALMOST ENTIRELY through a GENERIC RESPONSIVENESS prior (some genes move under many different knockouts): J-mover-frequency alone AUPRC 0.559 (8.95x, feature importance 0.78), while the G-SPECIFIC mechanistic layers ALONE (regulon+RWR+network+reaction, no prior) are ~CHANCE at 0.066 (1.06x), and combining EVERYTHING adds only +0.008 AUPRC over the prior. So the predictable part of the knockout response is gene-intrinsic RESPONSIVENESS, not knockout-specific MECHANISM. This CORRECTS the earlier loose 'cascade ~ chance': the cascade is 9x-predictable, but the mechanistic far-field (which of THIS knockout's specific targets move) does NOT compose for unseen knockouts (GATA1's 9.2x regulon was the well-powered exception, not a composable rule). CAVEAT: J-mover-frequency is computed cross-fold (leave-self-out but across all knockouts), so the prior's absolute AUPRC is mildly optimistic; the leakage-FREE result is the G-specific-only 1.06x = chance, which is the clean measurement of the wall. The fair, exhaustive, combined shot -- taken and measured
    "wholecell.json",              # PRODUCT (wholecell.py, 'wholecell'/'endtoend' syscall): the WHOLE steady-state model chained end-to-end and MEASURED -- the culminating integration. genome -> regulation (promoter POLR2A at TSS = k_txn) -> mRNA level ([mRNA]~k_txn/k_deg, k_deg from the sequence decay model halflife.py) -> protein level ([protein]~k_transl*[mRNA]/k_prot_deg, translation+degradation from protein_abundance.py), scored at every hand-off and end-to-end vs measured K562 mRNA (RNAdecayCafe RPKM) + protein (cell-image ppm), chromosome-GroupKFold, 7,515 genes. RESULT (the steady-state chain COMPOSES): STAGE1 regulation->mRNA promoter-only r 0.29 -> +decay model r 0.60; STAGE2 mRNA->protein oracle (measured mRNA+sequence) r 0.71 (ceiling); END-TO-END genome/promoter->PROTEIN with NO measured mRNA: promoter-only 0.15 -> +decay 0.61 -> FULL CHAIN r 0.633 (R2 0.40). So from genome/regulation to protein the assembled chain retains r=0.63 held-out, close to the oracle ceiling 0.71 -- the gap is the promoter->mRNA hand-off (a single Pol II track is a lossy transcription-rate proxy). Each layer adds (decay is the big jump because [mRNA]=k_txn/k_deg needs BOTH transcription and decay). This is the part of the cell that COMPOSES quantitatively. The MUTATION arm (NEXUS mutation -> network propagation -> cascade) is SEPARATE and unchanged: near-field validated (regulon 9.2x), far-field is the measured wall (does not compose). Honest bottom line: the steady-state abundance cell is a real end-to-end quantitative predictor (genome->protein r 0.63); the dynamical far-field cascade is still the wall
    "viper.json",                  # RESEARCH (viper.py, 'viper'/'activity' syscall): roadmap Point 4 -- regulator ACTIVITY from the target FOOTPRINT, not its own abundance (VIPER/DoRothEA aREA logic on the canonical CollecTRI signed regulon), validated on Perturb-seq. Fixes 'activity!=abundance' in principle: a TF/kinase can be fully transcribed yet functionally silent, and its mRNA does not tell you; aREA reads activity off whether the regulon moves coherently. NES_TF = sum_t mode_t*rank_norm(s_t)/sqrt(n_t) over targets in the KO signature. VALIDATION = knock out TF X -> X's activity (from its TARGETS, zero info from X's own mRNA) should be among the most-inactivated of ~259 regulons. HONEST NEGATIVE, precisely diagnosed: recovery AUC 0.564 vs shuffled-regulon control 0.495 (0.5=chance), true TF in top-10 only 6.6%. WHY (not a coding bug, the method is faithful): (1) 97% of pseudobulk TF-KO footprints are near-empty (<5 movers at |z|>2) -- nothing to read; (2) where a footprint exists the PAN-TISSUE regulon mis-signs the K562 response (GATA1 regulon sign-agreement 0.33 < chance -- predicts the WRONG direction); (3) |NES| is confounded by regulon size (r=0.64, big pan-tissue regulons 'recover' via diffuse noise). This is the project's recurring wall in the INFERENCE direction: the generic regulon does not carry K562-specific wiring. NOTE it DOES work where signal exists -- 'viper GATA1' returns MYC/E2F inactivated (cell-cycle arrest) + SPI1 activated (the GATA1-SPI1 antagonism), biologically correct. The fix is a K562-SPECIFIC regulon. INFERENCE (data->activity), the reverse of forward prediction; does not by itself break the forward far-field wall
    "regulon_learn.json",          # RESEARCH (regulon_learn.py): roadmap Point 1 -- a K562-specific, Perturb-seq-SUPERVISED regulon to fix the recall collapse (mutreg/propagate seed from TRRUST's ~66 curated targets and miss most movers). For the 8 erythroid-core TFs with genome-wide K562 ChIP (GATA1/GATA2/KLF1/MAX/MYC/RUNX1/SPI1/TAL1): candidates = genes whose PROMOTER (TSS+/-5kb) is ChIP-bound; label = moves in that TF's Perturb-seq KO (|z|>1.5); features = ChIP peak strength/proximity/count + promoter chromatin (DNase/H3K27ac/H3K4me3/POLR2A/H3K27me3) + baseline expression. Model learns WHICH bound genes are functional (binding!=function). HONESTY GATE = leave-one-TF-out vs a responsiveness prior (mechanism must beat 'some genes just move' to claim TF-specific generalization). HONEST NEGATIVE (untestable, not a coding bug): only 23 functional positives across all 8 TFs (mostly GATA1=19, MAX=4); 6 of 8 TF knockouts have ZERO promoter-bound movers, so <4 evaluable held-out TFs and the leave-one-TF-out AUPRC (mechanism 0.032 vs prior 0.006) is NOISE, not generalization. Two real causes, both the recurring wall: (1) these TFs act through DISTAL enhancers, not promoters, so promoter-binding is the wrong candidate set; (2) K562 Perturb-seq footprints are too SPARSE (same emptiness that sank viper.py). Recall-collapse is real but is NOT fixable by promoter-ChIP supervision on this dataset; NO regulon exported, NO generalization claimed
    "metabridge.json",             # RESEARCH (metabridge.py, 'metabridge'/'fbabridge' syscall): roadmap Point 5 -- replace the RWR far-field GRAPH WALK (which dilutes to 1.08x noise 4 layers deep) with a mass-balanced FBA METABOLIC BRIDGE, since the ONE place a bypass model is exactly computable is metabolism (matches REACTION_CHAIN.md's independent conclusion). Pipeline: knockout -> enzyme capacity (Human-GEM, GPR-aware) -> FBA reroute -> metabolite shift -> metabolite-sensing TF (curated literature map: cholesterol/SREBP, oxysterol/LXR, FA/PPAR, bile/FXR, retinoid/RAR, succinate-fumarate/HIF) -> near-field TRRUST regulon = predicted second wave (two accurate 1-step hops bridged by a metabolic solve, not a 4-step guess). MEASURED honest result -- correct architecture, LOCATES the wall (does not break it on this data), THREE quantified buffering layers: (1) only 625/2848 genes (22%) are a SOLE catalyst -- the rest have isozymes (OR-GPR) and lose ZERO flux; (2) even most sole-catalyst BIOSYNTHETIC enzymes lose nothing because the open-exchange model IMPORTS the product rather than making it (their reactions carry no WT flux; only a few like DHODH bite) -- so deterministic reroute barely fires without a constrained medium; (3) the metabolite->sensor->regulon CLOSURE is concrete and shown on STATED metabolite changes (curated map, decoupled from the non-unique/over-buffered pfba flux step) but is NOT broadly validatable because K562 Perturb-seq metabolic-KO transcriptional footprints are near-empty (1 of 1441 KOs moves >=10 genes) -- the ground truth for the readout is absent. Also honest: steady-state FBA gives fluxes+shadow prices, not concentration transients ('substrate spikes' need dFBA/kinetics). A real queryable pipeline + a precise negative: the bypass/redundancy problem is baked into metabolism itself
    "pathway_ko_verify.json",      # RESEARCH (pathway_ko_verify.py): verify the LABELLED network against K562 Perturb-seq KO ground truth, PER EDGE TYPE -- for each labelled edge A->B with A knocked out, does B actually move? RESULTS (enrichment over base): curated regulatory (TRRUST TF->target) 6.0x = VERIFIED-real transcriptional edges (KO a TF -> curated targets move); coexpression 1.44x + PPI 1.29x = WEAK-BUT-REAL; co-dependency 1.15x; BULK inferred regulatory (10,376 of the 612k reg edges) 0.97x = NOISE/chance (most reg labels do NOT predict KO effects -> low-confidence layer); complex co-membership 0.0x, pathway co-membership 0.0x, signaling 0.0x = NOT TRANSCRIPTIONAL (knocking out one member of a complex/pathway does NOT move the others mRNA -- these mark protein/functional modules, not transcriptional co-response); 0 pathways show member co-movement above threshold. This is a direct QC identifying WHICH labels are trustworthy for KO prediction (the CURATED regulon) vs structural-only (complex/pathway/PPI) vs low-confidence (bulk inferred reg). Aim-1 answer (how a KO affects the network): the perturbation propagates through DIRECTED regulatory edges, not physical/membership structure -- verified real there (6x), decorative/structural elsewhere. Both verifies the network and locates the KO-transmitting layer
    "fullstack_run.json",          # PRODUCT (fullstack_run.py): the whole current stack run on 60 HELD-OUT knockouts at HONEST deployment (train on the other ~1790, score EVERY gene -- no subsampling inflation). RESULT refines both earlier overstatements: (1) MAGNITUDE predicted-vs-actual response size Spearman 0.459 (improved connectivity model) -- usable order-of-magnitude how-disruptive. (2) IDENTITY: top-10 deployment precision SCALES WITH KNOCKOUT STRENGTH -- STRONG KOs (>=50 movers, n=7) get 9.0/10 in the top-10 on REAL full-universe deployment (RPL27 10/10, RBM25 10/10, EIF2S3 9/10), WEAK KOs (<15, n=48) get 0.81/10, average 2.02/10. So the 9/10 is REAL for strong knockouts (NOT a pure subsampling artifact as I over-corrected last turn); the subsampling had inflated the AVERAGE to look uniformly 9/10; and in all cases the hits are GENERIC usual-suspect genes (a strong KO moves so much of the transcriptome that predicting the usual movers lands), never protein-specific. (3) NEAR-FIELD: only 4 panel KOs are curated TFs, regulon top-10 0/10 (too small to conclude). BOTTOM LINE at scale: tells you HOW BIG (Spearman 0.46) and WHICH genes at a precision scaling from ~9/10 (strong KO) to ~1/10 (weak), always generic stress genes not protein-unique. Corrects BOTH the inflated 9/10 and the too-harsh ~1/10 retraction -- the honest deployment number is strength-dependent.
    "celltype_id.json",            # RESEARCH+CORRECTION (celltype_id.py): the HONEST held-out recalculation of 'can we guess exactly which cell'. The coherence test's 78-80% was FAMILY matching (same lineage) and NOT held-out. Proper test: split emask genes into two independent halves, build each cell type profile from half-A and half-B separately, query half-B against all 200 half-A profiles. Out of 200 cell types (chance 0.5%): by GENE FUNCTION (GO-BP) EXACT top-1 26%, top-3 48%, top-5 56%, family/lineage top-1 68%, median rank of the correct cell 3 of 200; by PROTEIN TYPE (GO-MF) exact top-1 18%, top-5 50%. HONEST READING: from the functions/types of a cell's expressed genes we get the right FAMILY ~68% of the time and the EXACT cell as #1 ~26% (usually near the top, median rank 3/200, top-5 56%) -- far above 0.5% chance but NOT the ~80% I earlier implied (that was family-level, non-held-out). Exact-top-1 loses points on near-identical sub-types (memory vs effector CD8 T, monocyte subsets) sharing most genes. Corrects the overstated coherence headline: "right family almost always, right exact cell often and near-top otherwise."
    "celltype_coherence.json",     # RESEARCH+POSITIVE (celltype_coherence.py): the user's cross-modal 'reverse it' idea -- take each cell type's EXPRESSED genes (emask, 200 atlas cell types), look at the FUNCTIONS (GO Biological Process) of those genes, and ask if that reconstructs the cell's own identity; then pair honestly with protein + 3D. RESULT: the mRNA->function->identity loop CLOSES. (Q1) marker cell types recover the RIGHT cell-SPECIFIC biology from their expressed genes' functions -- hepatocyte->xenobiotic/fatty-acid/steroid metabolism+bile-acid+coagulation; CD4 T cell->TCR signaling+adaptive immunity; neuron->synaptic transmission+axon guidance; monocyte->antigen processing+MHC; cardiac muscle->sarcomere+force-of-heart-contraction. (Q2 DECISIVE) cell identity is RECONSTRUCTABLE from function ALONE: each cell type's nearest neighbour by expressed-gene-function profile shares its lineage 78% of the time vs 11% chance = lift 7.08x (997 GO-BP terms). So reversing expression->function regenerates cell functional identity; the mRNA layer and gene-function layer are mutually CONSISTENT. HONEST LIMITS on the other two requested pairings: (Q3) PROTEIN -- we have only a GENERIC proteome (one ppm per gene, NOT per cell type); active genes are 2.88x background abundance but a true per-cell-type mRNA<->protein comparison is impossible without cell-type proteomes (real gap; HPA/Tabula proteomes would fill it). (Q4) 3D CHROMATIN -- only K562 Hi-C exists and missing_wire_3d already showed TAD co-localisation is a weak wire (0.016); per-cell-type 3D for the 200 atlas types is not in our data. BOTTOM LINE: where the model IS cell-type-resolved (mRNA + gene-function) it is internally coherent and reconstructs identity (lift ~7x); it is NOT cell-type-resolved for PROTEIN or 3D chromatin -- those are the honest missing modalities to acquire. NOTE: caught+fixed a marker-display bug (first pass ranked terms vs global -> showed housekeeping 'translation' for every cell type; fixed to cross-cell-type specificity ranking; Q2 was always correct since it uses the full profile).
    "fullstack_celltype.json",     # RESEARCH (fullstack_celltype.py): the user's idea -- run the SAME 60-KO deployment as fullstack_run but ACTIVATE THE NETWORK ONLY FOR K562-ACTIVE GENES (baseline expression clean_mean>0.05 in the K562 data itself; 8561 of 16492 network genes; every network feature -- PPI degree, regulon, coexpr/codep degree, centrality, near-field flag -- and the candidate universe restricted to K562-active, masking the ~half of the graph not expressed in K562). RESULT = ~NEUTRAL on native K562 deployment: top-10 1.98->2.03 (+0.05), STRONG 9.0->9.29 (+0.29, ~7 KOs so within noise), WEAK 0.77->0.79, magnitude Spearman 0.459->0.426 (-0.033) -- all within xgboost run-to-run noise (~±0.1 top-10). (An earlier too-aggressive threshold clean_mean>0.2 HURT because it dropped ~48% of real movers from the universe; even at 0.05, 37% of real movers are lowly-expressed and become unreachable -- masking by expression inherently loses some movers.) TWO honest reasons it is a wash HERE: (1) the deployment universe was ALREADY cell-type-specific -- it scores each KO's MEASURED (=K562-expressed) genes, so inactive genes were never candidates; masking mostly changes network-DEGREE features the magnitude model already handles; (2) the forecast's dominant signal is the generic expression/abundance prior, not fine graph topology. STRATEGIC POINT: cell-type activation is the CORRECT, principled thing to do and cannot hurt much, but its real payoff is for TRANSFER to a DIFFERENT cell line (mask to THAT line's active subgraph) -- exactly where the cross-line result (reproduce_rpe1: K562->RPE1/HCT116 rho 0.13) says cell-type specificity actually bites. On K562's own data the readout was already K562's expressed transcriptome, so masking is redundant.
    "sciplex_time.json",           # RESEARCH (sciplex_time.py): the closest available TIME-RESOLVED test of the transient hypothesis. No sub-24h GENETIC perturb-seq exists as a clean h5ad (would need GEO MTX assembly; the Aissa scPerturb file turned out to be single-timepoint), so used Sci-Plex 3 which has TWO timepoints -- 24h (acute) and 72h (adapted). Only A549 has BOTH (K562/MCF7 are 24h-only), so ran in A549: 47 drugs with both timepoints, streamed the 2.5GB CSR, delta vs time-matched vehicle. RESULT: the response RESHAPES massively 24h->72h -- profile Spearman only 0.062, mover-set Jaccard 0.128, 77% of 24h movers are TRANSIENT (gone by 72h) and 77% of 72h movers are ADAPTIVE (new by 72h). So a single steady-state snapshot MISSES a large amount of time-specific structure = direct support for the transient hypothesis behind the compensation wall (transients/buffering a late single timepoint cannot contain). NOISE CAVEAT (honest): for weak drugs the 24h/72h mover sets are near-noise so the 77% is likely INFLATED by per-drug pseudobulk noise (same as the saturation test); but near-zero profile correlation even over the union of movers + the biology (24h acute vs 72h survivor state) support a genuine large reshape for responding drugs. SECOND CAVEAT: 24h-vs-72h CHEMICAL (A549), NOT the 2-12h GENETIC window where paralog compensation is expected -- shows time-resolution MATTERS and transient/adaptive movers are real+large, but a proxy not the exact experiment. The exact sub-24h genetic perturb-seq remains the real highest-value unmet fetch (GEO). BOTTOM LINE across the 3 requested sources: time-resolution demonstrably matters (this), but the ideal dataset to break the transient-compensation wall is not cleanly available; 3D-TAD chromatin (missing_wire_3d) and primary-CD4 transfer (cd4_transfer) were both honest negatives.
    "cd4_transfer.json",           # RESEARCH (cd4_transfer.py): transfer test to a PRIMARY, non-cancer cell type -- Shifrut-Marson 2018 primary human CD4+ T-cell CRISPR-KO perturb-seq (scPerturb; 52,236 cells, 20 immune-gene KOs + control; the tractable stand-in for the 22M-cell CD4 screen, which is impractical to stream). CRITICAL: 0 knockouts are shared with our K562 set (CD4 perturbs T-cell-signalling genes PDCD1/LAG3/CD3D/CBLB..., none in the K562 genome-wide set), so a same-KO reproducibility test (like RPE1/HCT116) is IMPOSSIBLE; tested generic transfer instead. Q1 TIDE CONSERVATION (is the generic 'usual suspect' program the same leukemia vs primary T cell): Spearman -0.065 (~0, 0/50 top-usual-suspect overlap vs 0.5 chance) = the generic tide does NOT transfer to a primary immune cell AT ALL, unlike between cancer lines (K562<->RPE1<->HCT116 which shared the program). Two honest reasons: the CD4 screen perturbs T-cell activation genes (very different biology from ribosome/proteostasis essential-gene stress) AND primary T cells have a distinct baseline transcriptome -- so BOTH the cell type and the perturbation biology differ. Q2 MAGNITUDE TRANSFER: CD4 response size (n_movers) still tracks K562-derived essentiality (Spearman 0.286) and PPI degree (0.40), so the magnitude intuition (essential/central genes -> bigger response) DOES carry across cell types (n=20, small). NET: the model's generic tide is a cancer-cell-stress program, NOT a universal one -- transfer to a functionally-distinct primary cell type is weak for the response program but the magnitude/importance axis generalises. Reinforces (strongly) the cross-cell-line finding: the response is heavily cell-type-conditioned; only the size-vs-importance relationship is portable.
    "missing_wire_3d.json",        # RESEARCH+NEGATIVE (missing_wire_3d.py): the 3D-chromatin follow-up to missing_wire.py, using REAL K562 Hi-C TADs (ENCODE ENCFF271SAF, in-situ Hi-C Arrowhead contact domains, 5703 domains GRCh38) instead of the crude +/-1Mb linear proxy. Tests whether a gene is a tide 'usual suspect' because it shares a physical 3D TAD neighbourhood with other high-tide genes. RESULT = weak/negative: predicting per-gene tide score (4337 genes across 1923 TADs, median 1 measured gene/TAD), real TAD-neighbourhood tide density scores Spearman 0.016 (corr 0.038 ~0), BELOW the PPI control (0.091) and even below the linear proxy (0.039); it adds only +0.017 beyond expression(0.194)+PPI and +0.018 beyond the linear proxy. So real 3D TAD co-localisation is a real but SMALL signal, barely better than 1D genomic proximity and far short of a dominant wire. Combined with the promoter-ATAC negative (missing_wire.py), the physical-genome hypothesis for the tide is only weakly supported at TAD resolution -- the 'usual suspects' are best explained by baseline EXPRESSION + network connectivity, not chromatin geography. HONEST CAVEATS: median 1 measured gene/TAD limits power; a finer test (shared stress ENHANCERS within a TAD, or per-cell Perturb-ATAC) remains open, and Perturb-ATAC is NOT cleanly available as an h5ad (scPerturb has none; ENCODE has perturbation-snATAC but not as a ready h5ad).
    "analogy.json",                # PRODUCT+POSITIVE (analogy.py): THE combination test, and the FIRST real partial breach of the far-field wall in the whole investigation. Since reproducibility proved the far-field is REAL signal (weak-KO movers recur rho~0.25 within line) that the PPI graph cannot read, predict a held-out knockout's movers by ANALOGY -- borrow from its most FUNCTIONALLY-SIMILAR other knockouts (k-NN/collaborative filtering; similarity built ONLY from response-independent annotation: shared protein complexes, pathway, process, coexpression + codependency partners, PPI neighbourhood -- never the held-out KO's own response). Deployment on 1160 K562 KOs, K=15 neighbours. RESULT survives the decisive RANDOM-neighbour control: TOP-10 precision ANALOGY 0.307 vs RANDOM-neighbours 0.103 vs generic PRIOR/tide 0.168. DECISIVE metric -- recall@50 of the SPECIFIC movers OUTSIDE the global tide top-100 (which the climatology prior CANNOT get, =0.0 by construction): ANALOGY 0.288 vs RANDOM 0.044 (+0.244 -- a 6.5x gain from FUNCTIONAL similarity, NOT from averaging strong profiles) vs PRIOR 0.0. Predictions are genuinely KO-specific (cross-KO overlap 0.232 vs the old forecast's 0.779). So the reproducible-but-unpredicted far field IS partly readable -- the right lens was ANALOGY to functionally-similar knockouts (co-complex/pathway partners share each other's specific movers), NOT the protein graph. A real, buildable gain worth wiring into the forecast. HONEST caveats: absolute specific-recall ~29% (modest, wall bent not broken); it is a functional-NEIGHBOURHOOD signal not per-gene mechanism; similarity includes coexpr/codep (baseline data, not KO-response, so not circular, but response-adjacent -- an annotation-only complex+pathway+PPI variant would be even cleaner). This is the payoff of the 'signal is real, graph is the wrong lens' reframe.
    "missing_wire.json",           # RESEARCH+NEGATIVE (missing_wire.py): tests the hypothesis that the reproducible generic TIDE (usual-suspect movers) is routed by OPEN CHROMATIN (real K562 ATAC-seq, ENCODE IDR peaks, promoter signal) rather than protein circuits (PPI/regulon), since PPI already fails to predict it. Target = per-gene tide score (mover frequency across ~2000 K562 KOs). Held-out Spearman by feature group (7211 genes): baseline EXPRESSION 0.15, PPI/regulon control 0.108, ACCESSIBILITY (ATAC+enh) only 0.068, neighbourhood/TAD-proxy 0.05; full model 0.219. HONEST NEGATIVE on the promoter-ATAC version: accessibility is the WEAKEST group, does NOT beat PPI or expression, and adds only +0.018 beyond expression+PPI. Strikingly ATAC promoter signal correlates NEGATIVELY with the tide (-0.095) -- the most-open promoters are HOUSEKEEPING genes that are STABLE and do NOT move (opposite of 'open doors get bound'); tide movers are inducible/poised stress genes, not constitutively wide-open ones. Top importance = PPI degree (0.31), then expression, then regulon; ATAC/enh near the bottom. TWO honest caveats keep the broader idea alive: (1) NO static per-gene feature explains the tide well (full model only 0.22) -- the 'usual suspects' are only weakly a fixed per-gene property; (2) only promoter ATAC + a crude genomic-neighbourhood TAD PROXY were tested -- real Hi-C 3D TAD co-localisation and the metabolic-messenger (Acetyl-CoA/ATP->HAT->global chromatin) wire were NOT tested and remain open (the honest next fetch). NET: 'open chromatin routes the tide' is falsified for promoter accessibility (it underperforms and anti-correlates); the tide stays weakly-and-best explained by network connectivity + expression; the 3D-Hi-C and metabolite versions remain untested.
    "sciplex_saturation.json",     # RESEARCH (sciplex_saturation.py): the SATURATION INDEX test via graded chemical titration -- Sci-Plex 3 (Srivatsan-Trapnell 2020, downloaded from scPerturb/Zenodo; K562/24h, 188 drugs x 4 doses 10/100/1000/10000nM, streamed the 800k-cell CSR, restricted to the ~8.4k genes shared with our K562 KO set). HONEST SPLIT RESULT. Q1 CONFIRMED: response MAGNITUDE scales with dose -- median Spearman(log dose, n_movers) = 0.40, 71% of drugs have magnitude rising with dose. So response SIZE is a graded, dose-tunable quantity, exactly as the capacity/saturation model predicts, extending the K562-knockout magnitude finding (~0.5) to graded chemical perturbation. Q2 NOT SUPPORTED: no dose-invariant program -- low-vs-high profile Spearman 0.007 (~0), and even between the two HIGHEST both-responding doses only 0.022 (~0). I initially expected the same program to just scale with dose; the data does not show that. Either the program reorganises with dose, or (more likely) per-drug pseudobulk profiles are too noisy at these doses for a stable-program rank-correlation (only HDAC inhibitors Panobinostat/Dacinostat/Quisinostat/Givinostat + CDK Flavopiridol drive big responses). NOT claiming program stability. NET: Sci-Plex confirms MAGNITUDE is dose-tunable (the predictable axis) but does NOT demonstrate a fixed dose-invariant identity program -- 'dose predicts how big', 'which genes' stays the hard part, consistent with the whole investigation.
    "reproduce_rpe1.json",         # RESEARCH (reproduce_rpe1.py): DOWNLOADED a THIRD cell line from scPerturb (Zenodo) -- Replogle-Weissman 2022 RPE1 (non-cancer retinal epithelium, 2394 CRISPRi KOs x 247,914 cells; streamed the gzip-dense matrix in cell-blocks and pseudobulked). RPE1 is SAME lab + SAME CRISPRi protocol as our deep K562, so K562-vs-RPE1 is the CLEANEST cross-cell-line test (protocol held fixed). RESULT: 1126 shared KOs, median z-profile Spearman 0.132 (STRONG 0.24, WEAK 0.089), top-20 mover overlap 1.6/20 -- essentially IDENTICAL to the cross-protocol K562-vs-HCT116 (also 0.132). This is the key control: removing the protocol confound did NOT raise cross-line reproducibility, PROVING the ~half loss of signal across cell lines is REAL cell-type biology, not batch/protocol artefact. Confirms + sharpens reproduce.py: within-line reproducibility 0.32 vs cross-line 0.13 regardless of protocol; STRONG KOs (conserved essential machinery) transfer best, WEAK KOs near the floor (0.089) = cell-type-specific. NET: multi-cell-line data is essential CONDITIONING signal (a K562-trained far-field predictor transfers only weakly to another lineage), but there is no cell-type-invariant weak-KO far-field. NOTE: the CELLxGENE Census route was checked FIRST and does NOT carry guide-labelled perturbation screens (all 2203 catalog datasets searched -- matches were cell atlases, not screens); scPerturb/Zenodo is the correct source and works via direct HTTPS through the proxy.
    "norman_gi.json",              # RESEARCH (norman_gi.py): the genetic-interaction / compensation testbed, on Norman 2019 (K562, 106 single + 131 DOUBLE CRISPRa perturbations, all doubles have both singles + 11,855 controls; pseudobulked by streaming the 2.9GB CSC matrix). Tests Compensatory Shunting where it can actually be seen -- combinatorial perturbations. Q1 PREDICTABILITY: the ADDITIVE model (double ~= sum of singles) predicts the real double at Spearman median 0.403 and recovers 37% of the double's top movers -- so combinatorial responses are LARGELY (not fully) predictable from singles = a real, useful capability number for combo screens. Q2 COMPENSATION/EMERGENCE: only 28% of a double's movers are EMERGENT (move in double but NEITHER single), and 15% of doubles are strongly non-additive (additive rho<0.3); the top genetic-interaction pairs are functionally-redundant partners (KIF18B_KIF2C two kinesins, PLK4_STIL centriole, BCL2L11_BAK1 apoptosis) -- the biology validates. VERDICT: buffering/genetic interaction is REAL and measurable in COMBINATORIAL data (unlike the steady-state single-KO knockdown data where paralog compensation was at chance), but it is the MINORITY of the signal, concentrated in specific redundant/interacting pairs -- NOT a large hidden reservoir that would lift weak SINGLE-KO prediction to 6-7/10. USE: (1) additive-from-singles is a decent combo predictor (rho 0.40); (2) the non-additive minority is where a dedicated GI model adds value and Norman is its label set; (3) corroborates from a THIRD independent angle that single-KO steady-state compensation is not a big recoverable signal -- buffering appears only when you actually remove both copies.
    "reproduce.json",              # RESEARCH (reproduce.py): uses the OTHER downloaded perturb-seq datasets (genome-wide K562 = gwps; HCT116 colon) to answer THE decisive question -- when the SAME gene is knocked out in two independent experiments, do the SAME genes move? i.e. is the far-field 'wall' real signal our features miss, or irreproducible noise. Metric honesty: |z|>1 mover% and fold-over-chance are threshold/scale-sensitive across datasets (HCT116 z is compressed, chance~0), so the fair metrics are rank-based -- z-profile Spearman + top-20 mover overlap. SAME CELL LINE (K562 deep vs gwps, 1208 shared KOs): median profile Spearman 0.323, and 8/20 of the biggest movers recur; STRONG KOs Spearman 0.53 (9.4/20), and -- KEY -- WEAK KOs STILL reproduce (Spearman 0.251, 7.2/20). CROSS CELL LINE (K562 vs HCT116, 1063 shared): everything ~halves -- Spearman 0.132 (STRONG 0.281, WEAK 0.075), top-20 overlap 2/20. INTERPRETATION (two-sided, and it CORRECTS the pure-noise framing): (1) the specific movers -- incl far-field, incl weak KOs -- ARE reproducible signal WITHIN a cell line (7/20 of a weak KO's top movers recur, far above chance), so the 1.06x graph-prediction wall is largely a FEATURE/MODEL gap (our PPI/regulon graph doesn't encode the reproducible paths), NOT a noise floor -- more hopeful than pure chaos; BUT the obvious features already failed (paralogs=compensation.json, graph propagation). (2) Much of the reproducibility is the GENERIC TIDE (same usual-suspect genes recur; graded Spearman only ~0.32). (3) The response is strongly CELL-TYPE-SPECIFIC -- K562->HCT116 halves reproducibility, and weak-KO cross-line hits the floor (Spearman 0.075) = context-specific. NET on 'why not use the other datasets': DO use them -- they (a) PROVE the response is reproducible real biology within cell line (relocating the wall from noise to missing-features), (b) QUANTIFY cell-type specificity (~half the signal is K562-specific) = the conditioning a cross-line model needs, (c) make the near-field+tide more trustworthy. They do NOT hand us a weak-KO far-field that transfers across cell lines (near floor). Multi-dataset data reframes the wall + enables cell-type conditioning; it does not by itself breach the far field.
    "compensation.json",           # RESEARCH+NEGATIVE (compensation.py): direct falsifiable test of the 'Compensatory Shunting' hypothesis (proposed mechanism to CRACK weak knockouts to 6-7/10) -- when you knock a redundant gene down, does its closest PARALOG upregulate / become one of the specific movers? METHOD honesty: first tried the on-hand ESM matrix as the 'continuous structural field' but a positive control killed it -- esm_normal.parquet is per-dimension STANDARDISED (per-dim mean~0 std~1), destroying cosine geometry (known paralogs RPL7/RPL7A cos 0.043, ACTB/ACTG1 0.024 vs random 99pct 0.063 = at chance); switched to a CURATED Ensembl BioMart paralog map (10,627 genes, correct where present: GATA1->GATA2/3/4/5). RESULT = clean NEGATIVE: of the 123 deep-k562 knockouts that have >=1 MEASURED paralog, LITERALLY ZERO paralogs cross the mover threshold (mover lift 0.0x, up-mover lift 0.0x; paralog upregulation above the KO's own drift -0.001 all / +0.017 weak = ~0). Even with real paralogs, knocking a gene down does NOT light up its paralog in this data. THREE reasons: (1) K562 Perturb-seq is CRISPRi knockDOWN (dCas9-KRAB repression), and the paralog/genetic-compensation pathway (transcriptional adaptation) is triggered by mutant-mRNA DEGRADATION which CRISPRi does NOT produce -- mechanism absent from the measurement; (2) single STEADY-STATE snapshot (~days) -- a transient compensatory paralog that fired early and returned to baseline cannot be in the data (calm-lake problem); (3) COVERAGE CEILING -- only ~123 of ~1123 knockouts (~11%) even HAVE a measured paralog, so paralog compensation could touch at most ~11% and structurally CANNOT lift the global average from ~2/10 to 6-7/10. BOTTOM LINE: the Structural Compensation Engine is biologically reasonable but has NO signal to learn/validate against in THIS data and too little reach to move the average even in principle; the weak-KO ceiling is a DATA/measurement limit (knockdown modality + steady-state timing) + a METRIC cap (a weak KO with 5 real movers can never exceed 5/10 on a top-10 list), not an architecture to out-engineer. Honest fix = better METRIC (recall@n_movers) + new DATA (time-resolved / true-knockout Perturb-seq), not a bigger model on this data.
    "strong_vs_weak.json",         # RESEARCH (strong_vs_weak.py): answers "what actually differentiates STRONG (>=50 movers, ~9/10 top-10) from WEAK (<15 movers, ~1/10) knockouts?" -- measured on deep k562, 189 strong vs 1412 weak, medians + Mann-Whitney. Strong KOs separate on THREE axes (and NOT a fourth). (1) ESSENTIALITY by DEGREE: dep_frac 0.99 vs 0.77 (the BINARY essential flag is ~1 for both groups -- nearly every profiled KO is essential-ish; separation is in degree of dependence, not yes/no). (2) PHYSICAL CONNECTIVITY: PPI degree 65 vs 13 (5.0x, p~3e-30), network centrality 8244 vs 2461 (3.3x), pathway membership 7 vs 2 (3.5x) -- strong KOs sit at physical/pathway hubs. CORRECTION to a prior guess: coexpression degree (12 vs 12, p=0.19) and codependency degree (7 vs 7) are FLAT -- transcriptional co-regulation breadth does NOT separate them; specifically physical-interaction + pathway centrality does. (3) FUNCTION: strong enriched for CORE GENE-EXPRESSION MACHINERY -- transcription 42% vs 20% (2.1x), translation 31% vs 13% (2.4x), 'other/peripheral' DEPLETED 18% vs 31%. Concretely strong = GATA1, RPL7A (ribosome), SUPT6H/POLR2K/MED9/MED30/INTS2 (Pol II/Mediator/Integrator), RBM22/AQR/SNRPD2 (spliceosome); weak = zinc-finger paralog families (ZNF100/133/207/253/468, ZMAT2) -- redundant, specialised, low-connectivity. Strong KOs are also themselves more highly EXPRESSED (11.8 vs 5.4 ppm, 2.2x) = abundant core machinery. MECHANISM (capacity model made concrete): essential + physically-central + core-machinery hub -> large CAPACITY DEFICIT in a fundamental process -> big generic stress response (many genes move, top-10 usual-suspects land = ~9/10); peripheral/redundant gene (a zinc finger with paralogs) -> BUFFERED -> few movers -> ~1/10. So strong-vs-weak = essential+PPI/pathway-central+core-machinery vs dispensable+peripheral+redundant, which is why response SIZE is predictable (Spearman ~0.5 from exactly these features) while specific mover IDENTITY is not -- size set by node importance, identity by chaos.
    "coverage_delivery.json",      # RESEARCH+CORRECTION (coverage_delivery.py): how many knockouts/mutations can the model DELIVER a useful result for -- and it forced an HONEST RETRACTION of the earlier '9/10'. That 93% (weather.py top-2%) was measured on a NEGATIVELY-SUBSAMPLED eval set (~13x enriched with movers, base 7.7%); it does NOT survive real deployment. Scoring EVERY gene per knockout (the real per-request scenario, full universe base ~1%), precision is top-1 15%, top-5 11%, top-10 9%, and 0% of knockouts reach a genuine 9/10 bar (P>=0.5 flags median 35 genes/KO at 2.9% precision). The 9/10 was a subsampling artifact, not a deployable capability -- predict10's ~1-2/10 was the honest deployment number all along. COVERAGE BY QUESTION: (A) MUTATION effect (does a variant break/destabilise a protein): ~proteome-wide (~16.5k genes, r~0.63), reliable almost always -- the real strength. (B) SPECIFIC direct targets of a knockout: only 10% (24 of 237 = curated TFs + some signalling) have the annotation for a specific near-field list. (C/D) downstream response: every KO gets a calibrated forecast + magnitude estimate, but top guesses are only ~9-15% precise on real deployment and the confident calls are generic climatology genes, not protein-specific. BOTTOM LINE for a researcher: near-always reliable for MUTATION effects; specific direct-target list only for ~10% (annotated TFs); broad downstream = calibrated-but-generic forecast whose top picks hit ~1-2/10. NOT a 9/10 per-request deliverable. Key honesty correction of an evaluation-artifact overclaim.
    "magnitude.json",              # PRODUCT (magnitude.py): tried to SOLVE the weak magnitude predictor (how big is the response). HONEST MATCHED RESULT on deep k562 (1851 KOs, held-out 5-fold Spearman): old features->n_movers 0.521, RICH features (adding regulon size, PPI/coexpr/codep degree, 2-hop reach, neighbour-degree centrality)->n_movers 0.558 = a real but MODEST +0.037 from connectivity/centrality. KEY CORRECTION: the old ~0.22 headline was a DIFFERENT, harder dataset (gwps, pre-filtered to responders = how-much-among-responders); on the FULL knockout spectrum here (incl. many non-responders) even OLD features reach 0.521 because essentiality cleanly separates big-vs-tiny response -- so most of the apparent 0.22->0.56 jump is DATASET not model; my actual contribution is +0.037. TWO of three fixes FAILED honestly: total transcriptional displacement (sum|z|) did WORSE than the plain n_movers count (0.498 vs 0.558), and coverage (n_measured) is constant on the dense matrix so it was never a confound. What worked = the features: top drivers dep_frac + log_centrality + ppi_deg + reach = essentiality + CONNECTIVITY (how important/central the node is). NET: magnitude is moderately predictable (~0.56) on the full spectrum, my connectivity features add a real modest +0.037, and it is likely near the static-feature ceiling; the heavy tail (a GATA1-scale surprise from a NON-essential gene) stays unpredictable because essentiality cannot see it. Weak spot improved modestly and honestly, not the leap it first looks like.
    "forecast_decompose.json",     # RESEARCH (forecast_decompose.py): capstone decomposition answering (a) is the weather forecast near or far field, (b) do we still need the magnitude model -- with a DECISIVE cross-knockout control. Q1: the forecast is NEAR-FIELD + CLIMATOLOGY with NO genuine specific far-field skill. Of confident-correct calls: 1% near-field, 63% climatology (usual-suspect genes), 36% look specific-far -- BUT that is climatology in disguise: the cross-knockout control shows the forecast predicts nearly the SAME gene ranking for ANY two knockouts (Spearman 0.779 between random KO pairs), i.e. it issues essentially the same which-genes-move list regardless of which gene you knock out. Its only KO-specific feature is the near-field neighbour flag (real 5.08x lift on near pairs); far genes are ranked by generic responsiveness (a per-gene property), so the 8.52x on specific-far pairs is the responsiveness gradient = finer-grained climatology, not KO-specific transmission (still walled). Q2: the forecast does NOT contain the magnitude -- summing its per-gene probabilities predicts response size at Spearman -0.063 (~zero) vs 0.113 dep_frac / ~0.22 full capacity model; the sum is dominated by universe size + constant climatology, not response magnitude. So we DO still need the separate (weak) capacity model for how big. BOTTOM LINE: the weather forecast is an honest NEAR-FIELD + CLIMATOLOGY predictor (calibrated + confident exactly where biology was already predictable, same list for every KO); it does not crack the specific far field and does not give the magnitude for free. Its value is honest calibration + a confident near-field/tide subset, not hidden far-field or magnitude skill.
    "weather.json",                # PRODUCT (weather.py): applied the WEATHER-FORECAST stance -- accept microstate chaos (cannot name the specific distal gene), deliver a calibrated probabilistic forecast instead. Held-out by knockout, 25k (KO,gene) pairs. RESULT -- it does NOT beat the wall on point prediction but passes the weather tests, giving the RIGHT OUTPUT FORM: (1) CALIBRATION -- the per-gene move-probability is WELL-CALIBRATED, ECE 0.006 (when it says p%, the gene moves ~p%: forecast 0.94->observed 0.94, 0.75->0.78, 0.02->0.02) -- HONEST probabilities, which point-accuracy metrics never showed. (2) CONFIDENCE -- an ABSTAINING forecast is precise: the top-2% most-probable genes move 93% of the time (12x base), top-5% 81%, top-10% 56% -- commit where sure, abstain elsewhere (the 1-in-10 was the wrong framing: it forced a blanket top-10 even where there is no signal). (3) HORIZON -- near-field genes (regulon/PPI) MOVE MORE (high base, lift 4.6x) vs far-field CLIMATOLOGY (lift 9.1x over a sparse base) = a base-rate effect not the far field being more predictable; both honestly forecast, neither predicts the specific gene identity. CONCLUSION: weather forecasting is the right PARADIGM -- the correct final FORM of CellOS far-field = an honest knockout WEATHER REPORT (calibrated per-gene move-probability + high-confidence committed subset + magnitude[how big the storm] + tide[which program] + explicit climatology-only far field). Not a GPS route -- a weather report.
    "gmaps.json",                  # RESEARCH (gmaps.py): tried the GOOGLE MAPS algorithm (Dijkstra shortest WEIGHTED path from the knockout, edges weighted by verified trustworthiness -- curated regulon/SIGNOR = fast roads, noisy bulk reg = slow) -- routing, NOT diffusion (RWR/PageRank already ~chance). 25k (KO,gene) pairs, 237 KOs, base 7.7%, held-out by KO. RESULT -- recovers the NEAR-FIELD then hits the SAME wall: distance-1 neighbours (the direct regulon) move most (10.1% vs 7.7% base) but hit-rate is flat ~base for distance>=2 (no monotonic closer=moves). Held-out lift: shortest-path closeness 1.19x vs 1.16x for the GENERIC control (closeness from a RANDOM knockout = pure centrality) = essentially the SAME, so the signal is just CENTRALITY re-labelled (close to THIS KO no better than close to ANY KO); and it does NOT beat the responsiveness prior (8.69x; sp+prior 8.77x = +0.08). WHY Maps fails where it succeeds on roads: Maps needs MEASURED travel times (=transmission coefficients, unmeasured here) and a trip is one physical route; without real weights shortest-path collapses to topological distance = centrality, and the far-field is a FLOOD (the generic tide from a global budget breach), not a route. Routing = the wrong algorithm; the far-field is weather, not traffic. Right tools stay capacity (flood size) + tide (which flood).
    "hub_test.json",               # RESEARCH (hub_test.py): stress-tested MORE regulatory hubs (145 TF knockouts) and it CORRECTED the GATA1-driven hypothesis on both counts. Q1 -- NO systematic TF-hub magnitude blind spot: most TF KOs have a TINY response (median TF moves 2 genes) while capacity predicts ~12 for everything, so it OVER-predicts the many weak hubs (pred/actual 3.14x ~ 2.81x non-TF) and UNDER-predicts the rare tail giants like GATA1 (722 movers, predicted ~10). GATA1 is a TAIL OUTLIER, not proof TFs are under-read; the real defect is capacity gives a relative RANKING, not the extreme tail of a highly-skewed response-size distribution. Q2 -- the near-field regulon does NOT fix hubs: adding a TF own curated regulon to the tide HURTS recall (48.5%->37.7%) and regulon-only recovers just 0.7% of a hub actual movers (the ~6x is per-target ENRICHMENT over a tiny base, NOT wave coverage). Most named TF KOs barely respond (MYC 4, STAT5A 0); GATA1 is the exception. Testing more hubs KILLED two plausible fixes (hub-correction, regulon-layer). Honest stack unchanged: magnitude = rough ranking that misses the tail; broad wave = generic tide even for hubs; regulon = tight near-field enrichment only; identity = abstain.
    "predict_ko.json",             # PRODUCT (predict_ko.py): end-to-end demonstrator -- "knock out protein X, what happens?" Runs the full mechanical stack for a chosen KO (MAGNITUDE from capacity, TIDE = generic-program wave, FLAVOUR = mechanical module, IDENTITY = ABSTAIN), then reveals the measured K562 answer. WORKED EXAMPLES: RPL7A (ribosomal, essential) -> stack WORKS: flags LARGE, predicted wave recovers 13/15 real movers, program correct (generic/quiescence). AQR (spliceosome) -> LARGE, 8/15 wave, program correct (generic). GATA1 (erythroid MASTER TF, DepMap-nonessential) -> honest FAILURE MODE: capacity under-predicts magnitude badly (~10 vs actual 722) because DepMap essentiality != transcriptional impact for a regulatory hub, and flavour missed. Honest behaviour: MAGNITUDE = rough RANKING (Spearman 0.22) not a calibrated count, under-reads regulatory hubs; TIDE recovers ~half the wave; FLAVOUR usually just generic stress; IDENTITY correctly ABSTAINED. Strong for essential metabolic/machinery KOs, weak for regulatory hubs, never names the specific ripple. The concrete face of the full stack.
    "full_stack.json",             # PRODUCT (full_stack.py): the definitive CellOS far-field predictor with REASONING OUT -- purely mechanical, all 824 knockouts, scored at the resolution each layer actually works. RESULT: MAGNITUDE (how disruptive a KO is) predictable at Spearman 0.224 from node capacity; TIDE (which wave of genes moves) -- the generic responsiveness prior recovers 48% of a KO's real movers @top-100 and 63% @top-200 (THIS is the far-field, predictable at the program level -- precision@10's ~2/10 scored the wrong unit); FLAVOUR (which panic-button module) called 64% by the mechanical capacity/function classifier, but layering it on the tide does NOT beat tide-alone on recall (48% vs 48%, wins in only 24% of KOs) so the module is ~a wash at a 100-gene budget; IDENTITY (the one specific distal gene) stays WALLED at ~2.04/10 precision@10. BOTTOM LINE: reasoning removed, the far-field = MAGNITUDE (capacity) + TIDE (generic program, ~half the movers @100 genes, ~two-thirds @200) + a modest capacity-driven flavour call; only the chaotic per-gene ripple is unpredictable. The honest measured ceiling -- a real predictor at the right unit, not the 1-in-10 the wrong metric implied.
    "reason_modules.json",         # RESEARCH (reason_modules.py): the whole stack + LLM reasoning re-run at the RIGHT UNIT (modules/programs, not genes). Strict held-out: reasoner sees each KO's identity/function/capacity + the 15 NMF program fingerprints, commits WHICH module(s) each KO fires BEFORE reveal; scored vs mechanical XGBoost module-classifier + generic tide, recall @ 100-gene budget. Panel = top-8 by response size (GATA1, RPL7A, spliceosome/ribosome essentials). RESULT -- reasoning does NOT help even at the module level, and slightly HURTS: recall tide-only 0.095, mechanical 0.094, REASONED 0.075; module-selection the biology reasoner hit the true dominant module 0/8 (0%) vs the mechanical classifier 62.5%. THE FINDING (EIF2S1 lesson at the program level): my biologically-'obvious' calls were WRONG on all 8 because a STRONG knockout's dominant module is NOT its functional pathway but a GENERIC one -- GATA1's dominant response is an inflammatory/generic module (not heme M10), RPL7A's is generic (not ribosome M01), the spliceosome KOs' is generic (not ISR M05). The mechanical classifier wins by learning 'big essential KO -> generic panic button' = exactly the CAPACITY MODEL's prediction (largest deficits breach the global budget -> same generic stress program regardless of gene). So reasoning about specific biology fights the capacity logic and loses at EVERY level (gene AND module). CAVEAT: panel = strongest/most-generic-dominated KOs; weaker specific perturbations (e.g. a single lipid enzyme) may let a reasoned module help -- the one place left to look. BOTTOM LINE: the recoverable far-field = the TIDE (generic dominant program) + capacity-driven mechanical module classifier; an LLM biology reasoner adds nothing there. Reasoning is for the NEAR-field (mechanism/mutations); the far-field belongs to capacity + the tide
    "farfield_modules.json",       # RESEARCH (farfield_modules.py): tested the 'predict the tide, not the ripples' reframe -- the far-field is not thousands of bespoke gene wires but a SMALL set of transcriptomic PROGRAMS (stress modules/panic buttons) tripped when a capacity deficit breaches a global budget. 1851 K562-deep knockouts x 8563 genes, 15 NMF modules. RESULT -- the reframe is SUBSTANTIALLY RIGHT (biggest positive of the far-field arc): (C1 MODULARITY) 15 programs explain 56% of response variance, dominant program covers 55% of knockouts -- knockouts fire shared programs, not bespoke responses. (C2 THE TIDE IS THE ANSWER, key correction) the generic prior top-200 recovers 63.3% of a typical knockout's ACTUAL movers (top-50 34.5%, top-100 48.4%) -- so precision@10 measured the WRONG UNIT: at the PROGRAM level the far-field is largely predictable (high RECALL); we were penalising it for missing per-gene identity it never gets. (C3 FLAVOUR) which module a knockout fires is predictable held-out at 64.0% vs 54.5% always-dominant baseline (+9.5pts real specific gain from capacity/function), BUT using the predicted module's genes alone gives LOWER overall recall (29.6%) than the generic tide (48.4%) -- so the module is an ADDITIVE flavour layer (recovers 25.9% of the specific residual the tide misses), NOT a replacement. CONCLUSION -- the honest, correct scope of CellOS's far-field: (a) response MAGNITUDE from capacity (Spearman 0.22, capacity_trigger); (b) high-RECALL PROGRAM-level prediction (generic tide recovers ~63% of movers @ top-200); (c) modest SPECIFIC module-flavour (+9.5pts, +26% of the tail). Only the individual unannotated distal gene IDENTITY (the chaotic ripple) stays walled by the measurement gap. Predict the tide, not the ripples -- and the tide is 63% recoverable
    "capacity_trigger.json",       # RESEARCH (capacity_trigger.py): tested the proposed Capacity/Saturation-Index model -- the claim that you can predict a knockout's response MAGNITUDE (not which genes) from the capacity deficit of the knocked-out node, bypassing the missing per-edge k_f. Response magnitude = n_movers / total stress; predictors = dep_frac (empirical saturation index) + abundance + centrality + half-life. RESULT -- a genuine, MODEST positive and the RIGHT reframing: response magnitude IS predictable held-out (5-fold Spearman 0.224 from capacity features; technical n_measured confound alone = -0.038, so it is NOT technical). Feature DIRECTIONS all match the model: essential/chokepoint nodes drive BIGGER responses (ess +0.189, dep_frac +0.113), and -- the non-obvious confirmation of the buffering mechanism -- HIGH ABUNDANCE drives SMALLER responses (log_ppm -0.100: more excess capacity = more buffered = smaller response, exactly Step 2). The model does NOT predict which far-field genes move (it concedes far-field = a generic stress program), so it does NOT bypass the which-genes wall; instead it EXPLAINS that wall (a generic panic button tripped by a global biomass/budget breach -> gene-nonspecific, matching all our data) and converts it into a correctly-scoped NEW capability the wall-chasing never gave: predict HOW DISRUPTIVE a perturbation is (response size / variant impact) from node capacity. Magnitude yes (Spearman ~0.22, ~5% variance -- modest but real & held-out), identity no. The metabolic Saturation Index (FBA flux / kcat*abundance) is the finer-grained version for the ~1,500 metabolic enzymes; dep_frac is the empirical proxy that generalizes to all knockouts
    "conserved_signal.json",       # RESEARCH (conserved_signal.py): the recommended lever -- use a SECOND cell line (HCT116 colon, genome-wide Perturb-seq) alongside K562 to test whether cross-cell-line CONSERVATION adds knockout-SPECIFIC signal or just re-selects generic movers. 1694 shared knockouts; movers = rank-based top-100 by |z| per line (datasets on very different scales, so absolute z-thresholds are unfair). RESULT -- the lever did NOT pan out; it CONFIRMS the wall from a second lineage: (Q1) responses are OVERWHELMINGLY cell-specific -- median top-100 Jaccard = 0.025 (~6 of 100 top movers reproduce across K562 and HCT116), which also FALSIFIES the CellOS-2.0 'cell-agnostic simulator' premise (you cannot read one line's KO response off another); (Q2) the ~6 conserved genes are the USUAL SUSPECTS -- responsiveness-prior 167.7 vs 91.1 for cell-specific movers (~2x more generic); (Q3) conservation enriches the KO's own mechanism ~2.1x (0.0146 vs 0.0068) -- REAL but off a NEGLIGIBLE base (98.5% of even the conserved core is still non-mechanism), and it does NOT translate: precision@10 vs the conserved target 0.98 (mechanism adds -0.01) vs single-line 2.73 (mechanism -0.07) -- mechanism ~0 either way. CONCLUSION: a second lineage confirms KO responses are lineage-specific and the conserved core is mostly generic; the 2x mechanism concentration is too small/non-actionable to build a predictor on. Genuinely useful NEGATIVE: a 'universal' cross-line predictor is the wrong frame -- the honest path is a high-precision ABSTAINING near-field tool per cell line, not a broad cross-line one. Far-field wall stands from a second cell type
    "reason_layer.json",           # RESEARCH (reason_layer.py): tested whether a BIOLOGY-EXPERT REASONING LAYER on top of the full stack improves the 10 predictions. Strict held-out protocol: a script dumps each knockout's identity + function + network context (curated regulon, signaling partners) + the full-stack candidate pool but HIDES the measured movers; the LLM reasoner commits a reranked top-10 per KO BEFORE any reveal; a second script reveals + scores. Panel = 6 well-powered K562-deep (thr 1.5) knockouts: GATA1 (erythroid TF) + 5 essential core-machinery genes (POT1/SUPT6H/CHMP3/RPL7A/AQR). RESULT -- reasoning does NOT help: reasoned 3.17/10 = generic prior 3.17/10, slightly BELOW mechanical full-stack 3.33/10 (adds -0.17 vs full-stack, +0.00 vs prior). And confident MECHANISTIC bets BACKFIRED: GATA1 (bet the canonical erythroid effector program ALAS2/GYPB/HEMGN/GP1BB) = 0/10 despite 295 genes moving -- almost none were erythroid targets; SUPT6H (bet histone genes, SPT6's known specialty) 4 vs prior 5; RPL7A (bet co-regulated RP genes) 6 vs 7. Where reasoning 'helped' (CHMP3 +2, AQR +1) it was luck reordering GENERIC prior genes (MALAT1/FTL/TPT1), not insight. Generalizes the EIF2S1 lesson: the transcriptomic readout is dominated by generic stress-responsive genes, so betting the biologically-CORRECT specific program strictly hurts. CONCLUSION: the far-field wall is a MEASUREMENT gap, not a reasoning gap -- a domain-expert reasoner with all context except the answer cannot beat the dumb prior, because the missing thing is the measurement of specific transmission, not its interpretation. Caveats: n=6 panel (-0.17 within noise), single-shot (iterative feedback would leak the test set), reasoner reranked a mostly-generic pool (tool-augmented reasoner might differ) -- but the direction is unambiguous and consistent with every prior result
    "predict10_deep.json",         # PRODUCT (predict10_deep.py): re-ran the full-stack 10-prediction forward test against BETTER-RESOLVED ground truth to test whether the ~1/10 ceiling is data-sparsity vs model. No raw single-cell K562 exists (our K562 Perturb-seq is already Replogle pseudobulk z-scores), so proxy = the DEEPER k562.h5ad essential screen (more cells/perturbation, wider z-range) vs shallow genome-wide gwps, + a mover-threshold sweep. RESULT (precision@10, full stack): gwps thr2.0 = 1.0/10 (median 6 movers) -> k562-deep thr2.0 = 1.33/10 (same threshold, deeper data alone = +33%) -> k562-deep thr1.0 = 2.04/10 (median 16 movers). So the ceiling IS partly a sparsity artifact -- with only ~6 measured movers a top-10 physically cannot score high; richer/better-powered ground truth ~doubles the absolute count. TWO honest caveats: (1) mechanism STILL adds ~0 at every config (-0.01 to -0.06 hits/10) -- the lift is entirely MORE true movers for the GENERIC responsiveness prior to hit, NOT new knockout-specific transmission; (2) a lower z-threshold also admits noise (easier targets), so part of the lift is not pure signal. NET: the 1/10 headline was depressed by pseudobulk sparsity -> honest number with deeper data is ~2/10, but it is still the generic prior doing the work; the far-field specific-transmission wall is unchanged. The lever that would raise the SPECIFIC count (per-edge magnitude from a degron/4sU time-course) remains the missing measurement
    "predict10.json",              # PRODUCT (predict10.py): THE full-stack forward test -- run the whole predictor end to end, emit 10 concrete predictions per knockout, reveal the measured K562 Perturb-seq answer, count how many are TRUE. Full-stack ranker = mechanism tiers (curated regulon, then CellOS-2.0 signaling->TF->regulon bridge) backed off to the leave-one-out responsiveness prior (held-out for the target KO); top-10; scored vs actual movers. HONEST RESULT (all 237 KOs, no cherry-pick): full stack 1.0/10 avg (median 1, best 7/10, 56% get >=1); prior-only 1.07/10 -- MECHANISM ADDS -0.06 hits/10, i.e. the entire CellOS-2.0 causal rebuild does NOT raise the absolute top-10 count over the dumb generic prior (its 6-13x-enriched members are less likely to actually move than the top usual-suspect genes, so tiering them in slightly displaces better bets). Panel: GATA1 2/10 (DPYSL2,BST2), RIPOR1 3/10 (PRG2,FTH1,MALAT1 -- all generic usual-suspects), EIF2S1 0/10 (predicted the textbook ISR targets ATF3/DDIT4/TRIB3/HSPA5 -- biologically RIGHT but the sparse pseudobulk did not measure them moving, so a 0 that is not wrong). The wall made maximally concrete: reliably ~1 correct in 10 and it is a GENERIC mover, not knockout-specific mechanism; specific mechanism has real enrichment but does not lift the absolute count. Also shows precision@10 is bounded by data sparsity (median KO moves only 6 genes at pseudobulk)
    "reaction_sim.json",           # PRODUCT+RESEARCH (reaction_sim.py): the CellOS-2.0 gated bipartite runtime loop, built BOTH ways and measured vs K562 Perturb-seq -- the honest test of the blueprint's central promise (does gating make the response predictable?). Typed causal graph (SIGNOR signaling/activity/complex + TRRUST regulon) masked by SPATIAL (compartment) + ABUNDANCE (K562-expressed: ppm>0 or rpkm>1, cond_network precedent) gates -> Operational Interactome, step-wise propagation from each KO (max 3 hops), mRNA readout at terminal TFs (respects the readout mismatch: only mRNA-terminal predictions scored). FOUR conditions {ungated,gated}x{boolean,magnitude} + shuffled-KO generic control. RESULTS: (Q1 GATING) spatial+abundance prune only 8.2% of state edges and barely move enrichment (ungated_magnitude 4.19x -> gated 4.21x) -- gating removes impossible edges, does NOT manufacture magnitude (matches cond_network 1.12x); the gates would matter more for cross-cell transfer, untested here. (Q2 TERMINATION LAW -- clear result) MAGNITUDE (attenuate by SIGNOR-confidence x per-hop, threshold, RANK) beats the blueprint's BOOLEAN (reach=full-strength, die-at-closed-gate): boolean over-propagates to a 265/KO unranked hairball at 3.1x; magnitude predicts 68/KO and its RANKED TOP-10 hits 13.61x vs boolean top-10 2.67x. My termination law is the one to ship. (Q3 FAR FIELD) honest split from the control: the FULL SET (4.21x) is barely above the generic full-set floor (3.37x) = mostly responsiveness prior, BUT the ranked TOP-10 (13.61x) is clearly specific vs the top-10 generic control (3.86x sd 2.9), ~3sd above -- so the confident TOP predictions are a REAL specific terminal-TF near-field reached via gated signaling hops, not a compounded far field. Only 31 KOs route to a terminal TF (scope = signaling-protein KOs, not genome-wide). CONCLUSION: adopt bipartite typed graph + cell-mask gates + MAGNITUDE (mass-action) termination -- a cleaner mutation-addressable Operational Interactome with a real near-field capability gain (specific top-10 for signaling-protein KOs); far-field magnitude past the terminal TF stays the measurement gap architecture cannot fill. Answers the blueprint honestly: gating helps precision, magnitude>boolean, far-field predictability is NOT recovered by architecture
    "reaction_graph.json",         # PRODUCT+RESEARCH (reaction_graph.py): started the TYPED CAUSAL REACTION rebuild -- graph as Entity--(effect/mechanism/residue)-->Entity instead of flat A--B lines. Sources fetched + parsed: SIGNOR 2.0 (43k signed causal relations, effect typed as activity-change[signaling] vs quantity-change[transcription] vs complex, + mechanism + RESIDUE + confidence), ComplexPortal (2,498 named macromolecular PRODUCTS each with GO function), Reactome (58MB interactions). Built 24,210 typed protein->protein causal edges mapped to our gene universe. VERIFIED vs K562 Perturb-seq, 3 tests + controls: (1) COVERAGE WIN (real): the typed causal core + products-with-function REPLACE the 612k bulk-inferred reg (measured 0.97x=chance) and upgrade flat PPI into DIRECTIONAL residue-resolved reactions (e.g. PTPN1 --dephosphorylation@Tyr1190--> INSR) -> mutation now lands on a specific residue of a specific reaction (feeds NEXUS). (2) THE SIGNALING->TERMINAL-TF->REGULON BRIDGE (the interesting positive, beat prediction): composing a SIGNOR signaling edge A-->TF with that TF's curated regulon predicts mRNA at 7.62x vs a 30-shuffle generic control mean 1.91x (sd 1.0) -- ~4x above generic (several sd out), mostly specific, AFTER excluding A's own direct targets. This is a genuine CAPABILITY EXTENSION: predict the transcriptional footprint of a SIGNALING-protein KO (a kinase/phosphatase has no regulon of its own) by routing signaling->terminal-TF->regulon -- flat regulon alone could not. Bounded: near-field (2 hops, terminal-TF-gated), DESCRIPTIVE (needs curated SIGNOR+TRRUST annotation), does NOT compound (generic hop-2 reach 1.79x). (3) HONEST LIMIT: direct per-class edge verification T1 is underpowered (only 69/136 SIGNOR edges intersect the CRISPR-KO set) so those 0.0x are small-N noise, not evidence. BOTTOM LINE: the rebuild is a genuine graph-quality upgrade (noise->signed/typed/residue causal core, mutation-addressable, real signaling layer, explicit universal/contextual split) that EXTENDS the trustworthy near-field one signaling hop upstream; it EXPLAINS the far-field wall rather than breaking it (edges carry sign+mechanism, not per-edge magnitude). Foundation of CellOS-2.0 bipartite rebuild
    "iterative_cascade.json",      # RESEARCH (iterative_cascade.py): the STEP-BY-STEP idea -- don't predict the far field all at once; take ONE validated near-field hop (the curated TRRUST regulon, ~6x), RE-LAY-OUT the graph (which predicted movers are themselves TFs = "carriers"), then take the NEXT hop from those carriers, and iterate -- scoring at EVERY hop to see exactly where a chained-1-step cascade holds vs dies. Discrete, SIGN-AWARE (compose activation/repression signs), TF-GATED (only carriers seed the next step). Pooled over 24 TF knockouts that have a curated regulon (17 reach scoreable steps), vs K562 Perturb-seq. RESULT -- it does NOT die the naive monotonic way; TWO things happen: (A) PRECISION COLLAPSES after hop 1 and never recovers: step1 (direct regulon) 2.1% precision @ 6.63x enrichment (small N, 2 pooled hits), then steps 2-4 sit at the ~0.5% floor. (B) ENRICHMENT does NOT decay -- it plateaus at ~3x (2.83x, 3.78x, 3.12x), but that plateau is the RESPONSIVENESS PRIOR re-entering by BREADTH: by step3-4 the fan-out balloons to a median 164->370 predictions and simply reaches generically-responsive HUB genes (move under almost any KO). Tells it is generic not specific: precision pinned at floor + SIGN accuracy drifts to chance/below (50%->67%->52%->44%, worse than a coin flip by step4 -> composed up/down direction NOT preserved). (C) CUMULATIVE RECALL reaches 32% by step4 but ONLY by carpet-bombing (~370 predictions) -- recall available only at <1% precision. BOTTOM LINE: step-by-step re-layout is MORE honest/interpretable than diffuse RWR (you watch confidence evaporate hop by hop) and confirms the wall from a new angle -- the ONLY confident hop is step1 (validated regulon); every later hop is precise-but-negligible or broad-but-generic; the chain never compounds into a correct far field because each discrete step carries sign+topology but not per-edge MAGNITUDE. Even step1 sign is at chance (small N), matching the earlier finding that the curated regulon predicts WHICH genes move (~6x) far better than the DIRECTION
    "cond_network.json",           # RESEARCH (cond_network.py): boosted the interconnections with WEIGHTS (reg-sign, coexpression strength, co-dependency strength, signaling), CONDITIONS (gate every edge on BOTH endpoints expressed in K562 -> remove edges that cannot fire), and RATES (target mRNA half-life = response time), then weighted-RWR vs the plain binary graph in the cascade_all held-out protocol (25k pairs, 237 KOs). Apparent result: enriched conditional-dynamic mechanism 3.64x vs plain graph 1.01x -- LOOKS like a big boost. DECOMPOSITION CONTROL (the honest test): the purely G-SPECIFIC relational part (conditioned/weighted edges + coexpr + codep) gives only 1.12x ~ plain graph, while the GENERIC per-gene features (half-life + expressed) alone give 3.62x. So the boost is ENTIRELY the generic half-life prior re-entering, NOT transmission. Conditioning/weighting/rating the edges sharpens edge quality and re-imports the generic axis but does NOT manufacture the per-edge TRANSMISSION coefficient. Same lesson as every enrichment, now controlled on the specific rates+conditions idea: the far-field wall is a MEASUREMENT gap (unmeasured per-edge transmission coefficient), not a graph-richness gap -- you can only READ the coefficient from a perturbation experiment, not compute it from a richer static graph. The G-specific-vs-generic split is what keeps this from looking like a win it is not
    "kinetics_prior.json",         # RESEARCH (kinetics_prior.py): tests the intuition "combine how-fast (mRNA half-life) with everything -> maybe it moves the cascade". Held-out cascade_all protocol, 20,351 (G,J) pairs, 237 KOs. FINDINGS: (Q1) half-life does NOT explain the responsiveness prior -- Spearman(half-life, movefreq)=0.007, so twitchy and short-lived are INDEPENDENT axes (intuition wrong there). (Q2) held-out lift: mechanism-only 0.99x (transmission=chance), half-life/kdeg-only 4.04x (its OWN measured, non-leaky generic predictor), movefreq-prior 8.3x (leaky), mechanism+kinetics 4.32x ~ kinetics-alone (mechanism adds nothing over it), full 8.64x (+0.34x over movefreq). VERDICT: a REAL but BOUNDED difference exactly where theory predicts -- half-life is a leak-free generic prior (honest for a genuinely-new KO where movefreq cannot be computed) and adds a small real gain, but it adds NO TRANSMISSION and does NOT break the wall, because half-life is a property of the TARGET gene J (how fast it tracks a synthesis change), not of the G->J edge (which genes a KO hits). Combining how-fast with everything cleans/augments the GENERIC axis + time-stamps the near-field (kinetics_ode), far-field which-genes stays the wall. The intuition was partly right (adds real signal) but lands on responsiveness, not transmission
    "kinetics_ode.json",           # PRODUCT (kinetics_ode.py): the mRNA-dynamics ODE (d[mRNA]/dt = k_syn - k_deg[mRNA]) parameterized with MEASURED K562 SLAM-seq rates (RNAdecayCafe, 8,867 genes) -- the DYNAMICS/response-time half of the cascade, which the steady-state models lacked. The mRNA half-life IS the response time (tau=1/k_deg: a gene changes only as fast as its old copies decay). Solver validated (Euler vs analytic err 1e-6). Response-time spectrum: half-life median 3.0h (q10 1.0 -> q90 8.4h); t90 median 10h. VALIDATED by biology: immediate-early genes turn over 6.4x FASTER than the genome (median 0.47h vs 3.0h, Mann-Whitney p=4e-9) -- built to respond fast exactly as tau=1/k_deg predicts; TFs also faster (1.9h vs 3.1h, p~0). APPLICATION = time-resolved NEAR-FIELD: for a TF knockout, its regulon (9.2x, predictable) members move on the timescale set by their measured half-lives (GATA1: NFE2 t90 2.6h, RUNX1/WT1 3.4h fast; others slow) -- WHICH direct targets move AND WHEN. Honest split (from the timesnap question): K562 SLAM-seq is resting-cell labeling kinetics (k_syn/k_deg), NOT a perturbation time-course; so this supplies the DYNAMICS half (how fast each gene moves) with real rates + validation, but NOT the TRANSMISSION half (which genes k_syn a knockout changes = the far-field wall). Upgrades the near-field into a time-resolved readout; cannot by itself break the far field
